WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.490 --> 00:00:08.430
- Welcome everyone.

00:00:08.430 --> 00:00:11.900
It is an absolute pleasure to introduce

00:00:11.900 --> 00:00:14.698
our speaker this afternoon,

00:00:14.698 --> 00:00:17.660
who is going to be part of our

00:00:17.660 --> 00:00:20.493
Distinguished Franke Speaker Series.

00:00:21.480 --> 00:00:26.073
And our guest today is no
one other than Carl Zimmer.

00:00:26.940 --> 00:00:30.560
Most recently, the author
of the book "Life's Edge,

00:00:30.560 --> 00:00:33.850
the Search for What it Means to be Alive."

00:00:33.850 --> 00:00:38.670
But before I tell you more
about Carl and the many books

00:00:38.670 --> 00:00:39.600
that he has written,

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:42.650
and the many ways in which his
writing in the New York Times

00:00:42.650 --> 00:00:46.530
over the past two years,
has enlightened, informed,

00:00:46.530 --> 00:00:51.530
and taught us about the COVID epidemic,

00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:56.780
how it's unfolding, what we
need to do to protect ourselves

00:00:56.780 --> 00:01:01.780
and the evolving, scientific
understanding of the pandemic.

00:01:02.250 --> 00:01:05.900
Let me start out first by
saying a few thank yous.

00:01:05.900 --> 00:01:09.411
I'd first like to
recognize our benefactors

00:01:09.411 --> 00:01:13.060
Mr. and Mrs. Richard and Barbara Franke,

00:01:13.060 --> 00:01:16.180
who have been ardent supporters

00:01:16.180 --> 00:01:19.310
of Interdisciplinary Activities at Yale,

00:01:19.310 --> 00:01:22.960
beyond the Franke program in
Science and the Humanities.

00:01:22.960 --> 00:01:24.660
We are deeply grateful to them,

00:01:24.660 --> 00:01:28.740
for the support of all
the exciting ventures

00:01:28.740 --> 00:01:33.740
that the Franke program has
undertaken since its inception.

00:01:34.350 --> 00:01:37.450
And I'm also delighted to report that

00:01:37.450 --> 00:01:39.040
they are in attendance today.

00:01:39.040 --> 00:01:41.300
So welcome Richard and Barbara.

00:01:41.300 --> 00:01:45.450
I also want to take this
opportunity to thank Ty Kamp

00:01:45.450 --> 00:01:50.050
for helping organize this
talk and all the series

00:01:50.050 --> 00:01:53.850
that we have been holding
so far and Guy Ortoleva

00:01:53.850 --> 00:01:57.440
for his help with recording
and making these recordings

00:01:57.440 --> 00:02:02.430
promptly available and
accessible to many people

00:02:02.430 --> 00:02:05.470
beyond those who can join us today.

00:02:05.470 --> 00:02:07.750
So we are recording this event.

00:02:07.750 --> 00:02:10.790
So I would like to all participants

00:02:10.790 --> 00:02:15.270
to leave your videos muted
for the duration of the talk.

00:02:15.270 --> 00:02:18.860
Please go ahead and submit
your questions at any time

00:02:18.860 --> 00:02:23.110
in the Q and A feature
on the chat and we will

00:02:23.990 --> 00:02:26.773
present these questions
to Carl at the end.

00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:32.320
So now let me move to
telling you a little bit more

00:02:32.320 --> 00:02:34.630
about our speaker today.

00:02:34.630 --> 00:02:36.550
So Carl, as you all know,

00:02:36.550 --> 00:02:39.600
reports from the frontiers of biology,

00:02:39.600 --> 00:02:43.440
where scientists are expanding
our understanding of life.

00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:47.320
And I mean, life in the
fullest possible sense.

00:02:47.320 --> 00:02:50.360
And the New York Times has called him

00:02:50.360 --> 00:02:53.710
the countries' most respected
science journalists.

00:02:53.710 --> 00:02:57.240
And many of you can
also attest to the fact,

00:02:57.240 --> 00:03:00.410
as I mentioned before,
that in the past two years,

00:03:00.410 --> 00:03:03.240
what he has done has been invaluable

00:03:03.240 --> 00:03:05.983
in helping us stay on top of COVID.

00:03:06.960 --> 00:03:09.800
He's been reporting for the
New York Times since 2004,

00:03:09.800 --> 00:03:11.120
where he now writes

00:03:11.120 --> 00:03:14.480
his wonderful weekly
column called "Matter."

00:03:14.480 --> 00:03:17.310
And his writing has won many, many awards,

00:03:17.310 --> 00:03:19.520
including The Stephen Jay Gould Prize,

00:03:19.520 --> 00:03:23.510
which is awarded by the Society
for the Study of Evolution

00:03:23.510 --> 00:03:26.530
in recognition for individuals
who sustain efforts

00:03:26.530 --> 00:03:29.690
of advanced public understanding
of evolutionary science.

00:03:29.690 --> 00:03:33.160
In 2021, he was a member of
the team at the New York Times

00:03:33.160 --> 00:03:35.930
that won the Pulitzer
prize in public service

00:03:35.930 --> 00:03:38.840
for coverage of the COVID 19 pandemic.

00:03:38.840 --> 00:03:41.530
Of course in addition to his reporting,

00:03:41.530 --> 00:03:45.747
Carl is the author of
14 books about science.

00:03:45.747 --> 00:03:47.650
This's just incredible.

00:03:47.650 --> 00:03:52.070
He's such a remarkable
writer with his insight,

00:03:52.070 --> 00:03:55.330
with his clear explanations, I mean,

00:03:55.330 --> 00:03:57.990
it's an absolute joy to read his books.

00:03:57.990 --> 00:04:00.660
And I have to gush here
that the first book of his,

00:04:00.660 --> 00:04:03.550
that I actually read
was the one on viruses,

00:04:03.550 --> 00:04:06.713
which of course I reread
during the pandemic.

00:04:07.610 --> 00:04:09.680
His latest book is "Life's Edge:

00:04:09.680 --> 00:04:11.720
The Search for What It Means to Be Alive"

00:04:11.720 --> 00:04:13.453
and it's a notable book,

00:04:15.230 --> 00:04:18.090
as picked by the New York Times for 2021.

00:04:18.090 --> 00:04:21.480
It was a Finalist for the PEN/E.O.

00:04:21.480 --> 00:04:24.540
Wilson Literary Science Writing Award.

00:04:24.540 --> 00:04:26.830
And so a little bit of his path.

00:04:26.830 --> 00:04:30.410
He started his journalism
career at discover,

00:04:30.410 --> 00:04:34.540
where he went on to work for
five years as a senior editor,

00:04:34.540 --> 00:04:36.290
he's written for many other magazines,

00:04:36.290 --> 00:04:38.310
including the "National Geographic,

00:04:38.310 --> 00:04:43.270
Wired, The Atlantic and in
2003, he launched the Loom,

00:04:43.270 --> 00:04:46.240
an award-winning blog, which
has been hosted by discover

00:04:46.240 --> 00:04:47.880
and national geographic.

00:04:47.880 --> 00:04:51.990
In 2015, Carl became a
contributing national correspondent

00:04:51.990 --> 00:04:55.210
for STAT, a publication
about health and medicine,

00:04:55.210 --> 00:04:56.253
where he hosted,

00:04:57.517 --> 00:05:00.570
"Science Happens," a video
series that was a finalist

00:05:00.570 --> 00:05:02.800
for a national magazine award.

00:05:02.800 --> 00:05:06.210
In 2019 Carl Zimmer created what is life,

00:05:06.210 --> 00:05:09.220
an eight episode series
of life conversations

00:05:09.220 --> 00:05:13.110
with leading thinkers about
why life exists, how it began,

00:05:13.110 --> 00:05:15.870
and other big questions about existence.

00:05:15.870 --> 00:05:19.690
He's a three time award winner
of the American Association

00:05:19.690 --> 00:05:22.460
for the Advancement of
Science Journalism Award,

00:05:22.460 --> 00:05:25.710
twice for his work on the New
York Times, once for the Loom,

00:05:25.710 --> 00:05:29.040
he's won the National Academy
Science Communication Award.

00:05:29.040 --> 00:05:32.030
So the list of awards just goes on and on,

00:05:32.030 --> 00:05:35.930
and I will sort of briefly
truncate it and just move on

00:05:35.930 --> 00:05:38.750
to tell you a little bit
more about his books.

00:05:38.750 --> 00:05:42.797
So in 1998, he published first book,

00:05:42.797 --> 00:05:46.040
"At the Water's Edge: Fish
with Fingers, Whales with Legs,

00:05:46.040 --> 00:05:49.030
and How Life Came Ashore
But Then Went Back to Sea"

00:05:49.030 --> 00:05:52.220
And since then he has
written these 13 more books,

00:05:52.220 --> 00:05:53.878
for which he has once again,

00:05:53.878 --> 00:05:56.720
won fellowships from the
Guggenheim foundation

00:05:56.720 --> 00:05:58.560
and the Sloan foundation.

00:05:58.560 --> 00:06:01.990
In 2018, he published, "She
Has Her Mother's Laugh:

00:06:01.990 --> 00:06:05.300
The Powers, Perversions,
and Potential of Heredity.

00:06:05.300 --> 00:06:08.670
The guardian named it the
best science book of 2018,

00:06:08.670 --> 00:06:10.570
and the New York Times
book review named it

00:06:10.570 --> 00:06:12.410
a notable book of the year.

00:06:12.410 --> 00:06:15.922
He's also won the 2019
Communications Award

00:06:15.922 --> 00:06:18.360
from National Academies
of Science, Engineering,

00:06:18.360 --> 00:06:21.863
and Medicine and Science and
Society Journalism Award.

00:06:22.830 --> 00:06:26.470
Amongst his other exciting books,

00:06:26.470 --> 00:06:30.750
the one that I have particularly loved,

00:06:30.750 --> 00:06:34.120
is the "The Tangled Bank: An
Introduction to Evolution"

00:06:34.120 --> 00:06:36.610
which was the first
textbook about evolution

00:06:36.610 --> 00:06:40.090
ever published, intended for non-majors.

00:06:40.090 --> 00:06:43.830
And it was named an
academic title of the year.

00:06:43.830 --> 00:06:46.400
One of the reasons I'm
sort of going through a map

00:06:46.400 --> 00:06:49.530
of his writing is to show you his range,

00:06:49.530 --> 00:06:54.530
depth and versatility in
terms of the kinds of topics

00:06:54.600 --> 00:06:59.600
and the genres in which he has
communicated so effectively

00:06:59.860 --> 00:07:03.470
with various sectors of the public.

00:07:03.470 --> 00:07:07.300
He also co-authored, "Evolution:
Making Sense of Life"

00:07:07.300 --> 00:07:10.460
which was now a textbook
for biology majors.

00:07:10.460 --> 00:07:12.990
And it was co-written with
the University of Montana

00:07:12.990 --> 00:07:14.163
biologists, Doug Emlen.

00:07:15.150 --> 00:07:16.880
And this is a book, they said textbook

00:07:16.880 --> 00:07:21.730
that is continually being
updated with new editions.

00:07:21.730 --> 00:07:24.393
And I think the fourth
edition is now in preparation.

00:07:25.300 --> 00:07:29.070
Carl is a popular speaker at
universities, medical schools,

00:07:29.070 --> 00:07:31.970
museums, and festivals as
you can probably imagine,

00:07:31.970 --> 00:07:36.610
high demand and has also,
you've probably also heard him

00:07:36.610 --> 00:07:38.773
on Radiolab on This American Life!

00:07:39.660 --> 00:07:44.610
So he is the only writer after whom

00:07:44.610 --> 00:07:49.610
both a species of tapeworm and
an asteroid have been named.

00:07:49.690 --> 00:07:54.040
So without further ado, as you can see,

00:07:54.040 --> 00:07:57.280
I'm very gushy about Carl's writing.

00:07:57.280 --> 00:07:59.900
I would, like to turn it over to Carl.

00:07:59.900 --> 00:08:03.490
Carl, we are delighted
to have you here today.

00:08:03.490 --> 00:08:04.390
- Thank you so much Priya.

00:08:04.390 --> 00:08:06.110
It's really great to be here.

00:08:06.110 --> 00:08:09.260
Thanks for having me, and
it's an honor to be part

00:08:09.260 --> 00:08:10.863
of the Franke Lecture Series.

00:08:12.210 --> 00:08:14.890
I wanted to talk today about

00:08:16.590 --> 00:08:18.740
life, I mean, as you had
indicated in your introduction

00:08:18.740 --> 00:08:21.650
there, life is kind of
my, as a journalist,

00:08:21.650 --> 00:08:23.190
is my beat as it were.

00:08:24.380 --> 00:08:27.710
And, but I wanted to
sort of talk about life

00:08:29.050 --> 00:08:31.490
in the big picture rather than,

00:08:31.490 --> 00:08:34.820
just one particular
fascinating aspect of it.

00:08:34.820 --> 00:08:38.653
So let me just share my screen,

00:08:39.810 --> 00:08:44.080
I also wanted to thank that
Franke for this poster,

00:08:44.080 --> 00:08:48.570
which is just gorgeous, and it's,

00:08:48.570 --> 00:08:49.810
sometimes you look at a poster

00:08:49.810 --> 00:08:52.080
and you're like, oh, they
picked some weird photograph

00:08:52.080 --> 00:08:53.710
just to catch your eye.

00:08:53.710 --> 00:08:55.920
But this actually has a
lot of meaning for me,

00:08:55.920 --> 00:09:00.360
and a lot of relevance to my
most recent book, "Life's Edge"

00:09:01.550 --> 00:09:06.430
because this thing that
you're looking at here,

00:09:06.430 --> 00:09:11.430
this is not out of some
science fiction movie.

00:09:12.040 --> 00:09:14.910
This is could be out of your backyard.

00:09:14.910 --> 00:09:18.140
This is an animal called the tardigrade,

00:09:18.140 --> 00:09:20.160
and here it's being photographed on,

00:09:20.160 --> 00:09:21.880
it looks like a bed of moss, probably,

00:09:21.880 --> 00:09:23.603
maybe grazing on a little algae.

00:09:24.980 --> 00:09:29.980
Tardigrade are incredibly common animals,

00:09:30.260 --> 00:09:34.910
but very weird animals and
weird in a way that's actually

00:09:34.910 --> 00:09:38.700
like helps us to really think about

00:09:40.600 --> 00:09:43.620
what we mean when we talk
about something being alive,

00:09:43.620 --> 00:09:45.043
like a tardigrade.

00:09:47.380 --> 00:09:48.213
So,

00:09:49.340 --> 00:09:50.173
the,

00:09:51.410 --> 00:09:55.430
so tardigrades, they're not
visible to the naked eye,

00:09:55.430 --> 00:09:56.563
they're very small.

00:09:59.782 --> 00:10:03.660
You need at least a crude
microscope to see them.

00:10:03.660 --> 00:10:06.670
And so people didn't know about that,

00:10:06.670 --> 00:10:09.330
were all surrounded by tardigrades

00:10:09.330 --> 00:10:11.723
until the 1770s Actually.

00:10:12.990 --> 00:10:13.920
There was a German

00:10:15.790 --> 00:10:20.700
preacher who named Johann August Goeze,

00:10:20.700 --> 00:10:22.400
I'm probably not
pronouncing that correctly,

00:10:22.400 --> 00:10:24.663
my German is not great.

00:10:25.610 --> 00:10:30.489
Anyway, so Goeze was a
preacher who in his forties

00:10:30.489 --> 00:10:32.608
discovered natural history

00:10:32.608 --> 00:10:36.920
and just jumped into
it with a huge passion.

00:10:36.920 --> 00:10:41.920
He actually collected,
he collected tapeworms

00:10:41.940 --> 00:10:45.990
and he had such a fantastic collection

00:10:45.990 --> 00:10:50.070
that the emperor of
Prussia bought it from him.

00:10:50.070 --> 00:10:51.840
I didn't know that Prussian emperors

00:10:51.840 --> 00:10:54.863
had an interest in tapeworm
collections, but this one did.

00:10:56.170 --> 00:11:00.510
In any case, he also
saw at a friend's house,

00:11:00.510 --> 00:11:05.510
a microscope and was
immediately taken with it.

00:11:05.820 --> 00:11:09.130
Microscopes had been in use
for a century at this point,

00:11:09.130 --> 00:11:11.130
but people were still using it

00:11:11.130 --> 00:11:12.290
to discover new things.

00:11:12.290 --> 00:11:14.949
And he got himself a microscope,

00:11:14.949 --> 00:11:17.380
And he got himself a microscope,

00:11:17.380 --> 00:11:19.330
this is actually his microscope,

00:11:19.330 --> 00:11:20.870
and started looking at stuff.

00:11:20.870 --> 00:11:23.500
A lot of what he would
look at would just be,

00:11:23.500 --> 00:11:26.450
water from a pond in his backyard

00:11:26.450 --> 00:11:27.563
and things like that.

00:11:29.630 --> 00:11:33.606
And he really marveled at this,

00:11:33.606 --> 00:11:38.510
he said a glass lens shows us a new world.

00:11:38.510 --> 00:11:41.400
And I know from experience that,
you'll be full of amazement

00:11:41.400 --> 00:11:43.790
when seeing that for the first time.

00:11:43.790 --> 00:11:48.790
He was really quite taken
with what he was seeing.

00:11:50.150 --> 00:11:53.380
And one of the things that really,

00:11:53.380 --> 00:11:57.800
really impressed him was this, (giggling)

00:11:57.800 --> 00:12:01.970
this is his, his own
drawing of this creature,

00:12:01.970 --> 00:12:03.433
which we call a tardigrade.

00:12:05.330 --> 00:12:08.990
Tardigrade is from the
French, it means slow walker,

00:12:08.990 --> 00:12:11.700
but actually Goeze didn't call it that,

00:12:11.700 --> 00:12:13.223
he called it a water bear.

00:12:14.430 --> 00:12:16.850
He said, "Strange is this little creature,

00:12:16.850 --> 00:12:18.780
because the whole organization of his body

00:12:18.780 --> 00:12:20.567
is extraordinary and strange."

00:12:22.030 --> 00:12:25.250
And he said that, "At
first sight has the closest

00:12:25.250 --> 00:12:27.350
similarity to a little bear.

00:12:27.350 --> 00:12:31.370
This also led me to give him
the name, little water bear.

00:12:31.370 --> 00:12:32.397
And then later he says,

00:12:32.397 --> 00:12:34.750
"One can hardly imagine
something more horrible

00:12:34.750 --> 00:12:37.070
than our little creature
appearing in the size

00:12:37.070 --> 00:12:38.207
of a real bear."

00:12:39.230 --> 00:12:41.030
That would be quite something.

00:12:41.030 --> 00:12:46.030
But in this world, water
bears are quite tiny.

00:12:46.350 --> 00:12:50.700
And so Goeze was observing
it through his microscope.

00:12:50.700 --> 00:12:55.700
And he was struck by these
eight little feet that it has,

00:12:56.250 --> 00:12:59.320
which are each armed with three claws.

00:12:59.320 --> 00:13:03.723
And it was moving around
under the microscope.

00:13:04.590 --> 00:13:07.130
He wrote his movements I
observed were always the same.

00:13:07.130 --> 00:13:09.770
He was on its back,
continually stretching out

00:13:09.770 --> 00:13:11.810
and retracting his feet as if he wanted to

00:13:11.810 --> 00:13:14.383
try to grasp something to get on his feet.

00:13:15.460 --> 00:13:18.530
So here you have a
naturalist who's observing

00:13:18.530 --> 00:13:20.673
a creature in action.

00:13:22.340 --> 00:13:25.073
The classic thing that
naturalists do with life.

00:13:26.370 --> 00:13:30.860
So now, we're, it's about 250 years later,

00:13:30.860 --> 00:13:33.760
and we know a lot more about tardigrades.

00:13:33.760 --> 00:13:38.510
We know that they are not just in ponds,

00:13:38.510 --> 00:13:42.080
not just on moss, there are
tardigrades in the ocean.

00:13:42.080 --> 00:13:43.480
There are tardigrades all over the place

00:13:43.480 --> 00:13:46.163
and they're incredibly
diverse group of species.

00:13:47.780 --> 00:13:52.150
And we can actually watch them walk,

00:13:52.150 --> 00:13:54.840
not just be on their back,
flailing their legs around,

00:13:54.840 --> 00:13:58.250
but this is actually
one of the first movies

00:13:58.250 --> 00:14:01.530
of a walking tardigrade.

00:14:01.530 --> 00:14:06.530
And they're not that slow for their size,

00:14:06.560 --> 00:14:09.723
they move pretty quickly
on those eight legs.

00:14:10.810 --> 00:14:12.129
So they clamber around.

00:14:12.129 --> 00:14:17.129
They are animals that move,
and they move towards things,

00:14:17.542 --> 00:14:19.413
usually they're moving to eat.

00:14:19.413 --> 00:14:21.430
They're finding something to eat,

00:14:21.430 --> 00:14:24.530
whether it's algae or
some single cell thing.

00:14:24.530 --> 00:14:29.160
And as tiny as tardigrade may be,

00:14:29.160 --> 00:14:32.773
they have a remarkable internal anatomy.

00:14:34.350 --> 00:14:36.210
They're surprisingly a lot like us

00:14:36.210 --> 00:14:39.793
as bizarre as they might
look on the outside.

00:14:40.740 --> 00:14:43.940
Sure, they have eight
limbs instead of four,

00:14:43.940 --> 00:14:46.650
but they have a brain
and they have a mouth

00:14:46.650 --> 00:14:50.310
and, they have a nervous
system and they have muscles,

00:14:50.310 --> 00:14:53.360
even if salivary glands and so on.

00:14:53.360 --> 00:14:56.350
You can see they have
reproductive organs as well.

00:14:56.350 --> 00:15:00.160
So here's yet another feature that shows

00:15:00.160 --> 00:15:03.580
that they are like us, living things.

00:15:03.580 --> 00:15:08.450
And living things as a rule reproduced,

00:15:09.490 --> 00:15:11.060
this is actually

00:15:13.120 --> 00:15:16.790
slightly scandalous video
of water bears having sex.

00:15:16.790 --> 00:15:18.313
This is how they do it.

00:15:19.510 --> 00:15:21.963
The male transfers sperm to the female,

00:15:21.963 --> 00:15:26.963
and then after fertilization
the female produces eggs,

00:15:27.850 --> 00:15:31.950
which it then lays, then
all those little eggs hatch,

00:15:31.950 --> 00:15:35.433
and now you have more tardigrades.

00:15:36.360 --> 00:15:40.180
So, I think we would all
feel very comfortable

00:15:40.180 --> 00:15:44.640
to say that tardigrades are alive.

00:15:44.640 --> 00:15:47.950
I doubt that there would be many people

00:15:47.950 --> 00:15:49.323
that would dispute this.

00:15:50.165 --> 00:15:52.040
It seems obvious.

00:15:52.040 --> 00:15:54.180
I mean we don't even have
to discuss what we mean,

00:15:54.180 --> 00:15:55.930
when we say that something's alive.

00:15:57.300 --> 00:16:01.139
And yet tardigrades can
do some remarkable things

00:16:01.139 --> 00:16:04.030
And yet tardigrades can
do some remarkable things

00:16:04.030 --> 00:16:06.300
that actually challenge

00:16:07.386 --> 00:16:09.633
our comfortable assumptions about life.

00:16:11.780 --> 00:16:14.647
So if you were to become dehydrated,

00:16:14.647 --> 00:16:18.040
So if you were to become dehydrated,

00:16:18.040 --> 00:16:22.720
let's say you lost 5% of
the water in your body,

00:16:22.720 --> 00:16:24.200
you would feel terrible.

00:16:24.200 --> 00:16:26.400
You lost 10%, you would feel worse.

00:16:26.400 --> 00:16:30.730
If you start losing 20% and
more you're going to die.

00:16:30.730 --> 00:16:32.523
That's it, you can't survive.

00:16:33.690 --> 00:16:36.830
And if I were to pour
water on you after that,

00:16:36.830 --> 00:16:40.671
you would not suddenly like
sit up and start talking.

00:16:40.671 --> 00:16:42.290
You're gone.

00:16:42.290 --> 00:16:44.003
You've died from dehydration.

00:16:46.060 --> 00:16:49.270
That's not true for the tardigrade.

00:16:49.270 --> 00:16:53.780
So the dehydration,
desiccation, drying out,

00:16:53.780 --> 00:16:57.110
that's a regular part
of being a tardigrade.

00:16:57.110 --> 00:17:00.423
It's just that they're in
environments often where,

00:17:01.440 --> 00:17:05.543
where they're going to get dried out.

00:17:06.890 --> 00:17:11.890
And when that happens, they
don't just become sort of

00:17:12.200 --> 00:17:13.740
a shriveled version of themselves.

00:17:13.740 --> 00:17:16.533
They go through a very remarkable change.

00:17:17.690 --> 00:17:20.840
So this is from an article,
an American scientist

00:17:20.840 --> 00:17:22.160
from a couple years ago.

00:17:22.160 --> 00:17:25.223
So on the top, it shows
a regular water bear,

00:17:26.070 --> 00:17:31.070
below it is a water bear
that has been dehydrated.

00:17:33.520 --> 00:17:38.163
And this strange little shape
it's called a tun, T-U-N.

00:17:39.270 --> 00:17:40.113
And,

00:17:43.550 --> 00:17:45.333
there's nothing going on in there.

00:17:47.100 --> 00:17:51.333
Tardigrades have to have to
breathe, these don't breathe.

00:17:52.330 --> 00:17:55.530
Tardigrades have to eat,
these tuns do not eat.

00:17:55.530 --> 00:17:58.360
They do not excrete
waste, they do not move,

00:17:58.360 --> 00:18:01.373
they look like a grain of sand.

00:18:03.810 --> 00:18:07.520
And yet, if you were to
pour water on that tun,

00:18:07.520 --> 00:18:10.620
it would swell up and it
go back to looking like

00:18:10.620 --> 00:18:12.520
the tardigrade grade at the top there.

00:18:13.690 --> 00:18:16.853
And you could wait quite
a while to do this.

00:18:19.000 --> 00:18:21.300
actually you could wait decades,

00:18:21.300 --> 00:18:23.413
and scientists have, they have,

00:18:24.450 --> 00:18:27.440
stored away tuns in collections.

00:18:27.440 --> 00:18:29.403
And eventually people have said,

00:18:30.725 --> 00:18:34.380
"Here's a tardigrade tun
that's been here for 30 years

00:18:34.380 --> 00:18:38.290
or 50 years, let's see what
happens if we put some,

00:18:38.290 --> 00:18:41.520
put it in water and more often than not,

00:18:41.520 --> 00:18:44.060
they're able to come back.

00:18:44.060 --> 00:18:46.033
And they really look very much like,

00:18:46.880 --> 00:18:48.690
just an ordinary tardigrade.

00:18:48.690 --> 00:18:53.043
Just walking around, eating,
just in the sense being alive.

00:18:56.590 --> 00:19:00.130
And what's incredible
is that this capacity

00:19:00.130 --> 00:19:05.130
it makes tardigrades
unbelievably tough, resistance.

00:19:05.870 --> 00:19:08.220
They can be blasted
with levels of radiation

00:19:08.220 --> 00:19:12.253
that would kill any other
animal many times over,

00:19:13.680 --> 00:19:17.640
they've been sent into space,
actually in this little craft

00:19:17.640 --> 00:19:22.640
they were shot into
orbit and the tardigrades

00:19:22.880 --> 00:19:25.130
that were sent up there
they were actually exposed

00:19:25.130 --> 00:19:28.243
to just the vacuum of space.

00:19:29.340 --> 00:19:32.070
And they wrote around
like that in outer space,

00:19:32.070 --> 00:19:36.300
no space suit and came back
to earth and then scientists

00:19:36.300 --> 00:19:37.850
were able to revive them again.

00:19:39.440 --> 00:19:44.440
So these tardigrades are one
of the most extraordinary

00:19:44.640 --> 00:19:48.100
creatures on earth and so
scientists are wondering,

00:19:48.100 --> 00:19:49.800
well, how do they do this?

00:19:49.800 --> 00:19:51.713
How is this possible?

00:19:53.640 --> 00:19:57.233
It's a very complex process
that they've evolved.

00:19:58.280 --> 00:20:01.260
So the first step is actually

00:20:04.670 --> 00:20:08.820
to avoid the damage that
comes with dehydration.

00:20:08.820 --> 00:20:10.460
So the problem with getting dehydrated

00:20:10.460 --> 00:20:12.993
is that all of our proteins and our DNA,

00:20:12.993 --> 00:20:17.993
they really need to be in
water to work properly.

00:20:18.250 --> 00:20:22.670
And so once water levels
start dropping down,

00:20:22.670 --> 00:20:24.590
proteins can get misfolded,

00:20:24.590 --> 00:20:27.142
there can be molecules
that clump together,

00:20:27.142 --> 00:20:29.713
and things just stop working.

00:20:30.670 --> 00:20:33.290
So tardigrades do this amazing thing

00:20:33.290 --> 00:20:35.580
where they they make a huge
amount of a special sugar

00:20:35.580 --> 00:20:40.210
come all trehalose and they
basically replace the water

00:20:40.210 --> 00:20:42.150
in their cells with trehalose

00:20:42.150 --> 00:20:44.617
and they can kind of
shrink their cells down.

00:20:44.617 --> 00:20:47.980
So this is part of the reason why,

00:20:47.980 --> 00:20:50.823
a tardigrade becomes really
tiny when it becomes a tun.

00:20:52.650 --> 00:20:54.830
But that's just the start actually.

00:20:54.830 --> 00:20:56.860
So they then, look at this diagram

00:20:56.860 --> 00:20:59.270
which is really complicated
is just reflects

00:20:59.270 --> 00:21:00.500
all the things that are happening

00:21:00.500 --> 00:21:03.853
inside of this animal's
body as it turns into a tun.

00:21:05.450 --> 00:21:07.350
You see a little line there vitrification.

00:21:07.350 --> 00:21:09.217
Vitrification you might
think of like glass,

00:21:09.217 --> 00:21:11.337
and that's kind of what happens actually,

00:21:11.337 --> 00:21:16.337
that it makes a bunch of
proteins that wrap around DNA,

00:21:18.060 --> 00:21:20.990
mitochondria, all sorts
of different structures,

00:21:20.990 --> 00:21:25.000
and freeze things in place
and protect them from damage.

00:21:25.000 --> 00:21:28.540
So essentially the tardigrade is becoming

00:21:28.540 --> 00:21:30.593
a kind of a protein glass.

00:21:33.160 --> 00:21:38.160
And like, so if we think of metabolism,

00:21:38.930 --> 00:21:41.513
for example, as being
like essential for life,

00:21:42.390 --> 00:21:43.740
these tardigrades aren't doing it,

00:21:43.740 --> 00:21:45.290
their proteins cannot move.

00:21:45.290 --> 00:21:47.593
They cannot react with
each other, that's it.

00:21:48.930 --> 00:21:52.493
And they can stay like that,
in this estate for decades.

00:21:53.510 --> 00:21:54.343
So,

00:21:56.483 --> 00:21:59.323
is the tardigrade that's
become a tun, is it alive?

00:22:00.470 --> 00:22:03.630
Well if we turn to
dictionaries for some help,

00:22:03.630 --> 00:22:06.530
it's a little difficult.

00:22:06.530 --> 00:22:08.430
So you have a definition here.

00:22:08.430 --> 00:22:10.480
The first one, the quality
that distinguishes a vital

00:22:10.480 --> 00:22:12.803
and functional being from a dead body.

00:22:14.160 --> 00:22:15.700
Well, what's that?

00:22:15.700 --> 00:22:17.930
I mean it's almost kind of circular.

00:22:17.930 --> 00:22:20.120
You're saying that life is something

00:22:20.120 --> 00:22:22.020
that living things have.

00:22:22.020 --> 00:22:22.853
Okay?

00:22:24.320 --> 00:22:26.440
The second one is a principle or force

00:22:26.440 --> 00:22:28.400
that is considered to underlie

00:22:28.400 --> 00:22:30.450
the distinctive nature of animate beings.

00:22:31.610 --> 00:22:32.523
What is that?

00:22:33.720 --> 00:22:36.723
This principle, it sounds
almost like alchemy,

00:22:38.140 --> 00:22:39.790
C is a little more concrete,

00:22:39.790 --> 00:22:43.300
an organismic state
characterized by capacity,

00:22:43.300 --> 00:22:47.670
for metabolism growth, reaction,
stimuli and reproduction.

00:22:47.670 --> 00:22:51.310
Okay, I mean, we can get
our heads around that

00:22:51.310 --> 00:22:53.470
scientifically, but does a
tardigrade tun metabolize?

00:22:53.470 --> 00:22:58.080
scientifically, but does a
tardigrade tun metabolize?

00:22:58.080 --> 00:22:59.363
Nope, can't eat.

00:23:00.290 --> 00:23:01.540
Can't produce waste.

00:23:01.540 --> 00:23:03.170
Doesn't do those reactions.

00:23:03.170 --> 00:23:04.130
They just do not happen.

00:23:04.130 --> 00:23:06.500
There's no water there
to allow them to happen.

00:23:06.500 --> 00:23:07.350
Can it grow?

00:23:07.350 --> 00:23:11.710
Nope, perfectly fixed in size.

00:23:11.710 --> 00:23:15.330
The cells do not divide because
they can't do metabolism.

00:23:15.330 --> 00:23:16.989
Do they react to stimuli?

00:23:16.989 --> 00:23:20.830
Well, with the exception of adding water,

00:23:20.830 --> 00:23:25.830
no, they just, it's essentially
like a grain of sand.

00:23:26.250 --> 00:23:27.340
Can they reproduce?

00:23:27.340 --> 00:23:28.283
Absolutely not.

00:23:31.490 --> 00:23:35.840
So, we're left here with a very
frustrating situation where

00:23:35.840 --> 00:23:38.950
we have something that
in its ordinary form,

00:23:38.950 --> 00:23:41.460
we would agree is alive, it's alive,

00:23:41.460 --> 00:23:43.780
but can go into another state.

00:23:43.780 --> 00:23:48.573
A state that really does not
fit our definition of life.

00:23:49.660 --> 00:23:53.709
I'm sort of reminded of all the terrors

00:23:53.709 --> 00:23:58.709
that humans have had about
being buried prematurely,

00:24:00.150 --> 00:24:03.715
because people are worried that
they might go into some sort

00:24:03.715 --> 00:24:07.010
of deep sleep or some type of state

00:24:07.010 --> 00:24:09.013
that would be mistaken for death.

00:24:11.100 --> 00:24:14.423
But in the case of tardigrades,

00:24:16.600 --> 00:24:19.940
it's very similar in some ways to death.

00:24:19.940 --> 00:24:24.320
Scientists don't call it death,
they call it cryptobiosis,

00:24:24.320 --> 00:24:25.783
which means hidden life.

00:24:27.514 --> 00:24:31.580
So cryptobiosis which other
animals can do as well,

00:24:32.460 --> 00:24:35.640
is in a way sort of taking life

00:24:35.640 --> 00:24:38.653
and sort of tucking it
away in a safe place.

00:24:39.740 --> 00:24:41.903
But the fact is that,

00:24:43.580 --> 00:24:48.410
by a lot of the sort of standard

00:24:48.410 --> 00:24:50.180
ways that we talk about life,

00:24:50.180 --> 00:24:54.383
a tardigrade can stop being
alive, and then be alive again.

00:24:56.810 --> 00:25:00.863
The there's another example, that's,

00:25:02.030 --> 00:25:04.843
it's also really fascinating
in a puzzling way.

00:25:04.843 --> 00:25:07.080
And that's viruses.

00:25:07.080 --> 00:25:10.730
This is a reconstruction
of the SARS-CoV-2 virus,

00:25:10.730 --> 00:25:13.380
which causes COVID 19.

00:25:13.380 --> 00:25:16.300
So if like me, you've had COVID,

00:25:16.300 --> 00:25:20.470
you had a billion of
these things inside of you

00:25:20.470 --> 00:25:21.560
replicating away

00:25:23.130 --> 00:25:26.560
and they're,

00:25:26.560 --> 00:25:28.170
tardigrades are small viruses

00:25:28.170 --> 00:25:29.823
are much, much smaller,

00:25:30.880 --> 00:25:35.880
but obviously, size doesn't
really take away from the,

00:25:36.630 --> 00:25:37.920
the power that they can have.

00:25:37.920 --> 00:25:42.090
I mean, this little virus
has turned our whole world

00:25:42.090 --> 00:25:45.723
upside down has wrecked Global economies,

00:25:46.864 --> 00:25:50.193
probably killed about 18
million people so far worldwide.

00:25:53.143 --> 00:25:57.367
And aside from the very
practical specifics

00:25:58.310 --> 00:26:00.913
of what viruses do to our lives,

00:26:02.010 --> 00:26:04.830
they're really very puzzling
in and of themselves.

00:26:04.830 --> 00:26:08.310
Like, what are these things?

00:26:08.310 --> 00:26:09.793
Are they alive?

00:26:11.720 --> 00:26:13.730
We couldn't even begin
to ask these questions

00:26:13.730 --> 00:26:16.320
until people had found viruses

00:26:17.450 --> 00:26:19.970
because viruses are so much
smaller than tardigrades.

00:26:19.970 --> 00:26:22.180
It took even longer for people to be aware

00:26:22.180 --> 00:26:25.390
that were surrounded with them,
that they're inside of us.

00:26:25.390 --> 00:26:27.907
So tardigrades were found in the 1770s,

00:26:30.210 --> 00:26:32.560
viruses really weren't recognized

00:26:32.560 --> 00:26:35.030
until for about a century later,

00:26:35.030 --> 00:26:37.060
a little over a century later.

00:26:37.060 --> 00:26:39.920
Obviously there were diseases like rabies

00:26:39.920 --> 00:26:42.080
and influenza that made people sick,

00:26:42.080 --> 00:26:46.713
but people couldn't say that's
a virus, that's causing it.

00:26:47.650 --> 00:26:49.930
That's something that
belongs to this class

00:26:49.930 --> 00:26:51.283
we're gonna call viruses.

00:26:53.030 --> 00:26:55.204
It actually wasn't a human virus,

00:26:55.204 --> 00:26:58.330
that was the first one
that was discovered.

00:26:58.330 --> 00:26:59.163
It was actually a plant virus.

00:26:59.163 --> 00:27:02.600
Tobacco farmers in Europe,

00:27:02.600 --> 00:27:07.350
we're dealing with a disease
called tobacco mosaic disease.

00:27:07.350 --> 00:27:11.490
And it would cause these
splotches that gave it its name

00:27:11.490 --> 00:27:12.653
kind of like a mosaic.

00:27:13.920 --> 00:27:16.843
And so some scientists
like Martinez Beijerinck

00:27:16.843 --> 00:27:19.193
And so some scientists
like Martinez Beijerinck

00:27:20.950 --> 00:27:23.620
figured out how to do an
experiment to understand

00:27:23.620 --> 00:27:25.070
what was causing the disease.

00:27:26.390 --> 00:27:29.780
They thought, well, maybe
it's a fungus or bacteria

00:27:30.700 --> 00:27:34.350
because by the late 1800s,
the germ theory of disease

00:27:34.350 --> 00:27:36.483
was becoming pretty well established.

00:27:38.270 --> 00:27:43.270
So what they did was they were
mash up a sick tobacco leaf

00:27:44.870 --> 00:27:46.610
and mix it with the water,

00:27:46.610 --> 00:27:49.370
and then pass it through a filter.

00:27:49.370 --> 00:27:54.010
So that big clumps of
stuff couldn't get through

00:27:54.010 --> 00:27:56.740
you just only small
things could get through.

00:27:56.740 --> 00:28:01.740
And they would inject it that
fluid into a healthy plant,

00:28:02.520 --> 00:28:03.770
and then they would see that actually

00:28:03.770 --> 00:28:05.373
that plant got sick as well.

00:28:07.120 --> 00:28:09.540
So that suggested, yes,
there was some kind of germ

00:28:09.540 --> 00:28:11.183
that was causing this disease.

00:28:12.130 --> 00:28:16.280
So then, Beijerinck actually
used another filter,

00:28:16.280 --> 00:28:18.003
a very, very fine filter.

00:28:18.920 --> 00:28:23.140
One that was where the pores were so small

00:28:23.140 --> 00:28:26.573
that bacteria and fungi
could not get through.

00:28:27.570 --> 00:28:29.300
So he did the same experiment again,

00:28:29.300 --> 00:28:31.090
mashed the lead, mixed it with water,

00:28:31.090 --> 00:28:32.350
pass it through a filter.

00:28:32.350 --> 00:28:34.450
Now he's got something that
really just looks like water,

00:28:34.450 --> 00:28:38.372
and he takes that and he
injects it into a leaf

00:28:38.372 --> 00:28:42.083
and lo and behold the leaf gets sick.

00:28:43.560 --> 00:28:47.160
So he's saying, well, there's
something else out there,

00:28:47.160 --> 00:28:50.320
something other than bacteria and fungi,

00:28:50.320 --> 00:28:52.113
I'm gonna call it a virus.

00:28:52.113 --> 00:28:55.440
So this is the first time
the word virus is used

00:28:55.440 --> 00:28:59.640
to describe these invisible
agents of disease.

00:28:59.640 --> 00:29:03.590
Couldn't see them, it's
not like he was Goeze

00:29:03.590 --> 00:29:04.740
looking through a microscope

00:29:04.740 --> 00:29:06.930
at his little lovely little water bears.

00:29:06.930 --> 00:29:10.200
He just, he could see the
effects of the viruses,

00:29:10.200 --> 00:29:12.150
but they were too small for him to see.

00:29:15.987 --> 00:29:20.400
This remained the case for decades.

00:29:20.400 --> 00:29:22.210
Viruses remained invisible,

00:29:22.210 --> 00:29:24.690
scientists were now able though to

00:29:24.690 --> 00:29:28.260
conceptually understand viruses

00:29:28.260 --> 00:29:30.943
and could then look for human viruses,

00:29:32.610 --> 00:29:36.470
Like for example, influenza.

00:29:36.470 --> 00:29:37.303
And,

00:29:39.220 --> 00:29:44.010
and so still though, the
microscopes of the early 1900s

00:29:44.010 --> 00:29:46.580
while they were much better
than the ones that Goeze had

00:29:46.580 --> 00:29:48.880
in the 1700s, they still
just weren't powerful enough

00:29:48.880 --> 00:29:50.453
to see a virus.

00:29:52.040 --> 00:29:55.220
So Wendell Stanley had
this interesting idea.

00:29:55.220 --> 00:29:57.920
He knew that you could make crystals

00:29:57.920 --> 00:30:00.440
out of different substances,

00:30:00.440 --> 00:30:01.830
we're familiar with salt crystals,

00:30:01.830 --> 00:30:05.340
but you can actually make
crystals out of proteins too.

00:30:05.340 --> 00:30:06.730
They under the right conditions,

00:30:06.730 --> 00:30:08.550
they'll come together in this sort of

00:30:08.550 --> 00:30:10.820
some repeating pattern.

00:30:10.820 --> 00:30:14.180
And if a crystal gets big
enough, you can see it.

00:30:14.180 --> 00:30:18.050
You can shoot x-rays at it
and figure out its structure.

00:30:18.050 --> 00:30:20.240
So I thought, well,
there's some indication

00:30:20.240 --> 00:30:23.630
that viruses have protein in them, so,

00:30:23.630 --> 00:30:25.583
why don't I make a virus crystal?

00:30:28.290 --> 00:30:33.290
So he grows a lot of tobacco mosaic virus,

00:30:33.430 --> 00:30:37.280
and tobacco plants, concentrates them,

00:30:37.280 --> 00:30:41.540
and then he sees lo
behold that these little

00:30:41.540 --> 00:30:44.320
spiky shapes start to form.

00:30:44.320 --> 00:30:46.860
They start to become big
enough that he can see them

00:30:46.860 --> 00:30:50.560
with the naked eye and
he can collect these

00:30:50.560 --> 00:30:55.240
and store them just, on a shelf, in a jar.

00:30:55.240 --> 00:30:56.910
And it's kind of like, cryptobiosis,

00:30:56.910 --> 00:31:00.260
he can like take them off
the shelf months later

00:31:00.260 --> 00:31:05.260
and put them in water and let
them infect a tobacco plant.

00:31:06.130 --> 00:31:08.623
And they cause just as
much as disease as before.

00:31:13.306 --> 00:31:16.347
So, this idea that something
that seems like it's alive

00:31:17.187 --> 00:31:19.937
So, this idea that something
that seems like it's alive

00:31:20.940 --> 00:31:25.940
can be a crystal, like salt,
this is kind of mind blowing.

00:31:28.120 --> 00:31:31.203
And when Stanley published his results,

00:31:33.000 --> 00:31:34.340
the journalists

00:31:36.220 --> 00:31:38.830
reporting on this really had a hard time

00:31:41.040 --> 00:31:42.363
keeping their composure.

00:31:43.430 --> 00:31:46.010
So the New York Times,
they would talk about this,

00:31:46.010 --> 00:31:48.320
how this staggers the imagination,

00:31:48.320 --> 00:31:51.220
the viruses they're
neither dead nor alive,

00:31:51.220 --> 00:31:53.920
kind of like a tardigrade tun.

00:31:53.920 --> 00:31:56.310
And they seem to lie on that shadowy

00:31:56.310 --> 00:31:59.730
borderland where matter is
ready to leap into life,

00:31:59.730 --> 00:32:01.130
which is a line I just love.

00:32:03.690 --> 00:32:08.690
The subject really just for
years kept obsessing people,

00:32:09.970 --> 00:32:13.800
and actually in 1940, a few years later,

00:32:13.800 --> 00:32:17.910
there was another article
which had a section

00:32:17.910 --> 00:32:20.777
that was headed, "Can Crystals Live?"

00:32:21.770 --> 00:32:24.380
and they talked about
Stanley's experiment.

00:32:24.380 --> 00:32:26.410
And they asking, were
these crystals alive?

00:32:26.410 --> 00:32:29.720
Apparently no more so
than diamonds, glass, sand

00:32:29.720 --> 00:32:32.700
or other crystals with
which we are familiar.

00:32:32.700 --> 00:32:35.330
Yet when virus crystals
were put on a tobacco leaf

00:32:35.330 --> 00:32:38.160
that mosaic disease
spread like a slow fire

00:32:38.160 --> 00:32:39.530
over a whole field,

00:32:39.530 --> 00:32:43.093
just as if it had been
infected by living bacteria.

00:32:46.500 --> 00:32:50.290
So, very early on, even before
we really knew very much

00:32:50.290 --> 00:32:51.903
at all about viruses,

00:32:53.430 --> 00:32:57.690
people recognized that
they posed a huge challenge

00:32:57.690 --> 00:32:59.453
to our concept of life.

00:33:01.880 --> 00:33:06.060
So finally microscopes
caught up with the viruses.

00:33:06.060 --> 00:33:08.450
So electron microscopes made it possible

00:33:08.450 --> 00:33:11.920
to actually visualize
viruses for the first time.

00:33:11.920 --> 00:33:14.679
This is what tobacco
mosaic viruses look like.

00:33:14.679 --> 00:33:18.103
They have this interesting
cylindrical shape,

00:33:19.740 --> 00:33:20.990
what's inside of them?

00:33:20.990 --> 00:33:22.520
What are those cylinders made of?

00:33:22.520 --> 00:33:25.790
Well, Rosalind Franklin,
who might be best known

00:33:25.790 --> 00:33:27.883
for her work on the structured DNA,

00:33:28.840 --> 00:33:31.480
before that, had actually
made a name for herself

00:33:31.480 --> 00:33:34.410
by studying tobacco mosaic viruses.

00:33:34.410 --> 00:33:36.740
And actually doing really important work

00:33:36.740 --> 00:33:39.000
to figure out what are
these things made of?

00:33:39.000 --> 00:33:40.613
What are viruses made of?

00:33:41.780 --> 00:33:46.780
And it turns out that,
that a tobacco mosaic virus

00:33:47.370 --> 00:33:51.010
is made of protein here,
their blue and violet

00:33:51.010 --> 00:33:53.710
show different proteins that
are kinda locks together.

00:33:54.840 --> 00:33:56.960
And they hold

00:33:58.580 --> 00:34:03.210
genetic material, in the case
of tobacco mosaic viruses,

00:34:03.210 --> 00:34:05.363
that material is RNA.

00:34:06.730 --> 00:34:11.470
We keep our genes in DNA,
which is a double strand.

00:34:11.470 --> 00:34:14.270
There's some viruses that
also have double stranded DNA,

00:34:15.280 --> 00:34:17.870
but a lot of them, a
lot of viruses have RNA

00:34:17.870 --> 00:34:19.953
for genes instead, that's one strand.

00:34:20.970 --> 00:34:24.160
So COVID 19 is caused by an RNA virus.

00:34:24.160 --> 00:34:26.960
The flu is caused by an RNA virus.

00:34:26.960 --> 00:34:30.703
Tobacco mosaic disease is
also caused by an RNA virus.

00:34:31.790 --> 00:34:33.720
But what's really
interesting here is that,

00:34:33.720 --> 00:34:37.720
it's just, this protein is just a package

00:34:37.720 --> 00:34:41.400
and it's just carrying these genes.

00:34:41.400 --> 00:34:45.940
It doesn't even contain
enzymes that you need to copy.

00:34:45.940 --> 00:34:47.333
their genes like we have.

00:34:48.280 --> 00:34:50.023
It's just a delivery system.

00:34:52.210 --> 00:34:56.470
Now it turns out that that
shape of tobacco Mosaic virus

00:34:56.470 --> 00:34:58.517
is not the rule for viruses.

00:34:58.517 --> 00:35:01.350
is not the rule for viruses.

00:35:01.350 --> 00:35:05.570
And, as you can see here, in this diagram,

00:35:05.570 --> 00:35:10.460
you have viruses that
are like soccer balls.

00:35:10.460 --> 00:35:12.370
Some, some are like snakes.

00:35:12.370 --> 00:35:15.233
Some look like weird little spaceships,

00:35:16.230 --> 00:35:18.739
they're all very small,

00:35:18.739 --> 00:35:22.793
but they have this incredible variety,

00:35:24.050 --> 00:35:25.500
that could literally trillions

00:35:25.500 --> 00:35:26.840
of species of viruses on earth.

00:35:26.840 --> 00:35:29.690
We don't know, we've barely
scratched the surface

00:35:29.690 --> 00:35:31.669
when it comes to their diversity.

00:35:31.669 --> 00:35:32.502
(Carl coughing)

00:35:32.502 --> 00:35:34.170
But in spite of all the diversity,

00:35:34.170 --> 00:35:36.323
there are a lot of things that unite them.

00:35:38.130 --> 00:35:41.130
In some ways, if you've seen
one virus you've seen them all.

00:35:42.600 --> 00:35:47.120
Again in the sense that the
viruses are not like cells.

00:35:47.120 --> 00:35:50.080
So they're not little
chambers full of metabolism

00:35:50.080 --> 00:35:51.343
going on inside,

00:35:53.001 --> 00:35:56.010
a cell can grow, a cell can divide,

00:35:56.010 --> 00:35:58.250
a cell can copy it's own DNA.

00:35:58.250 --> 00:35:59.700
Viruses can't do any of that.

00:36:00.540 --> 00:36:05.540
They're just a protein shell
with genetic material inside.

00:36:05.900 --> 00:36:09.560
And so Peter Medawar a famous immunologist

00:36:09.560 --> 00:36:12.880
of the 20th century said
that they were simply

00:36:12.880 --> 00:36:16.293
a piece of bad news
wrapped up in a protein.

00:36:17.330 --> 00:36:19.730
That's a good way of
summing up what a virus is.

00:36:21.280 --> 00:36:25.180
So what this piece of bad news does

00:36:25.180 --> 00:36:30.180
is infect cells and get the
cells to make more bad news.

00:36:31.220 --> 00:36:34.360
In other words, the protein shell

00:36:34.360 --> 00:36:36.670
will have receptors on its surface

00:36:36.670 --> 00:36:40.320
that can let it latch onto a cell

00:36:40.320 --> 00:36:43.913
and deliver its genes inside the cell.

00:36:45.050 --> 00:36:47.770
And the cell's own
machinery reads those genes,

00:36:47.770 --> 00:36:50.690
makes proteins and those viral proteins

00:36:50.690 --> 00:36:53.030
basically take over the cell

00:36:53.030 --> 00:36:56.650
and cause the cell to make new viruses,

00:36:56.650 --> 00:37:01.363
which then escape the cell
to go in infect other cells.

00:37:03.090 --> 00:37:08.090
So just with real basics
there of what a virus is,

00:37:08.850 --> 00:37:11.540
we have the question again,

00:37:11.540 --> 00:37:16.540
as we had with tardigrades and
their tun is a virus alive?

00:37:18.890 --> 00:37:23.430
Now I showed you the
dictionary definition before

00:37:23.430 --> 00:37:25.410
scientists actually have,

00:37:25.410 --> 00:37:29.453
will come up with their
own definitions of life.

00:37:30.650 --> 00:37:35.440
And I would say that the most influential

00:37:37.300 --> 00:37:42.300
is one that a group of
scientists came up with for NASA

00:37:42.390 --> 00:37:44.653
sorry about that, in the early nineties.

00:37:46.530 --> 00:37:48.600
They were having a
meeting about exobiology,

00:37:48.600 --> 00:37:50.300
about finding life on other planets.

00:37:50.300 --> 00:37:51.710
And they were like, wait,

00:37:51.710 --> 00:37:55.513
we need to agree on what
we're looking for, right?

00:37:56.390 --> 00:38:00.490
And so they had a spent
all day arguing over this,

00:38:00.490 --> 00:38:02.810
argued over dinner, and
by the end of the day,

00:38:02.810 --> 00:38:03.870
by the end of the evening,

00:38:03.870 --> 00:38:07.283
they had settled on a working definition.

00:38:08.820 --> 00:38:13.140
I write about this whole process
in my book "Life's Edge."

00:38:13.140 --> 00:38:16.410
I said, "Life is a self
sustained chemical system

00:38:16.410 --> 00:38:19.007
capable of undergoing
Darwinian evolution."

00:38:20.000 --> 00:38:24.543
So short, sweet, clear, good writing.

00:38:26.095 --> 00:38:28.950
And as I say, very
influential, like you'll see

00:38:28.950 --> 00:38:32.220
this definition or versions
of it around a lot.

00:38:32.220 --> 00:38:36.830
So we have these viruses,
Darwinian evolution.

00:38:36.830 --> 00:38:38.476
That's a big part of the definition.

00:38:38.476 --> 00:38:40.003
They certainly are.

00:38:41.780 --> 00:38:43.773
We're dealing with the Omicron variant

00:38:43.773 --> 00:38:46.773
of SARS-CoV-2 right now,

00:38:47.760 --> 00:38:51.420
variants are produced through
evolution, through mutations,

00:38:51.420 --> 00:38:53.000
through natural selection.

00:38:53.000 --> 00:38:58.000
So now we know Omicron is far more fit

00:38:59.360 --> 00:39:03.460
than the first viruses
that emerged in China.

00:39:03.460 --> 00:39:07.570
It has evolved a lot, since
it became a human pathogen.

00:39:07.570 --> 00:39:11.300
So no question that viruses
are capable of Darwinian

00:39:11.300 --> 00:39:14.530
evolution, there are
the textbook case of it.

00:39:14.530 --> 00:39:18.980
However, what about being a
self sustained chemical system?

00:39:18.980 --> 00:39:21.440
Well, there are chemicals in it obviously,

00:39:21.440 --> 00:39:23.870
but self sustained, in the sense of like

00:39:23.870 --> 00:39:27.560
being able to feed itself
to expel ways to maintain

00:39:27.560 --> 00:39:29.720
an internal environment.

00:39:29.720 --> 00:39:31.460
Nope, can't do it.

00:39:31.460 --> 00:39:32.800
It just doesn't do it.

00:39:32.800 --> 00:39:37.710
So, here we have something
that straddles the definition,

00:39:37.710 --> 00:39:39.973
which meets a definition halfway,

00:39:41.340 --> 00:39:43.143
is it half alive?

00:39:44.334 --> 00:39:46.513
Can we have such a thing?

00:39:48.780 --> 00:39:52.880
Gerald Joyce is a scientist who

00:39:52.880 --> 00:39:55.530
helped to come up with his
definition and wrote about

00:39:56.880 --> 00:39:59.570
how they came up with a definition later.

00:39:59.570 --> 00:40:03.370
And a reporter was asking
him about that experience,

00:40:03.370 --> 00:40:04.933
and brought up viruses.

00:40:06.180 --> 00:40:08.713
And Joy said to the reporter,

00:40:09.800 --> 00:40:11.750
according to the working definition,

00:40:11.750 --> 00:40:13.950
a virus doesn't make the cut.

00:40:13.950 --> 00:40:17.343
So sorry, viruses, they're not alive.

00:40:19.780 --> 00:40:24.313
Now, when I talk about this
in my book or in lectures,

00:40:24.313 --> 00:40:27.923
Now, when I talk about this
in my book or in lectures,

00:40:30.120 --> 00:40:34.090
scientists sometimes will reach
out to me, and they'll say,

00:40:34.090 --> 00:40:35.960
well, of course Joyce is right.

00:40:35.960 --> 00:40:37.390
I mean, viruses are not alive.

00:40:37.390 --> 00:40:42.390
I mean they just don't
have those essential things

00:40:42.680 --> 00:40:44.323
that make life possible.

00:40:45.980 --> 00:40:48.610
And then I will also hear
from other scientists who say,

00:40:48.610 --> 00:40:49.590
well, this is ridiculous.

00:40:49.590 --> 00:40:51.550
Of course, viruses are alive.

00:40:51.550 --> 00:40:54.940
You can't understand them
without thinking about them

00:40:54.940 --> 00:40:56.913
as evolving living things.

00:40:57.760 --> 00:41:01.220
I literally got two emails in the same day

00:41:01.220 --> 00:41:03.890
where I was being told that viruses were

00:41:03.890 --> 00:41:06.113
and were not alive from scientists.

00:41:06.960 --> 00:41:09.573
So this is still a live wire issue.

00:41:10.520 --> 00:41:14.380
I think one of the most intriguing ways of

00:41:16.720 --> 00:41:19.770
addressing this and
challenging Joyce comes from

00:41:19.770 --> 00:41:21.353
someone named Patrick Forterre.

00:41:22.470 --> 00:41:24.583
He's a French virologist.

00:41:25.630 --> 00:41:29.490
And he, you could kind of think of the,

00:41:29.490 --> 00:41:32.380
of viruses according to Forterre,

00:41:32.380 --> 00:41:36.440
a little like the tardigrades
in the sense that like,

00:41:36.440 --> 00:41:39.830
tardigrades can be in
two different states.

00:41:39.830 --> 00:41:42.880
So you've got the regular
tardigrade walking around

00:41:42.880 --> 00:41:44.330
and then you've got that tun.

00:41:46.010 --> 00:41:48.783
So a virus that's floating
around outside of a cell,

00:41:50.780 --> 00:41:52.700
think of that as kind of like a tun,

00:41:52.700 --> 00:41:56.320
it's got a genetic
material inside and so on,

00:41:56.320 --> 00:41:59.290
but we wouldn't really not really alive,

00:41:59.290 --> 00:42:01.403
but once it gets inside of a cell,

00:42:02.530 --> 00:42:04.440
things are very different.

00:42:04.440 --> 00:42:07.730
It's not so much the
virus itself that's alive,

00:42:07.730 --> 00:42:12.730
but it is the merger of
the virus and the cell.

00:42:15.420 --> 00:42:19.610
So an ordinary cell,
Forterre calls it a Ribocell.

00:42:19.610 --> 00:42:21.950
And that it's because they have ribosomes,

00:42:21.950 --> 00:42:25.340
And that it's because they have ribosomes,

00:42:25.340 --> 00:42:27.580
which are these factories that they use

00:42:28.730 --> 00:42:31.793
to make proteins based on their genes.

00:42:33.080 --> 00:42:36.393
But the cell is making
proteins for its own needs.

00:42:37.965 --> 00:42:42.580
If a cell gets hot, it can
make proteins that will help

00:42:42.580 --> 00:42:44.720
to protect itself against heat.

00:42:44.720 --> 00:42:49.580
It can make proteins to
stick on its surface to grab

00:42:49.580 --> 00:42:53.450
different kinds of things
that it needs to survive.

00:42:53.450 --> 00:42:58.450
So a cell can look out
for itself as it were.

00:43:00.000 --> 00:43:04.584
Now, once a virus gets into a
cell, as I mentioned before,

00:43:04.584 --> 00:43:06.640
all of a sudden there are
all these virus proteins

00:43:06.640 --> 00:43:07.473
inside of it.

00:43:08.450 --> 00:43:10.860
And these viral proteins,

00:43:10.860 --> 00:43:13.823
completely change what the cell does.

00:43:15.240 --> 00:43:17.130
In the case of COVID For example,

00:43:17.130 --> 00:43:20.320
if you get COVID

00:43:20.320 --> 00:43:21.920
the cells that are infected

00:43:21.920 --> 00:43:24.020
with SARS-CoV-2 inside your body.

00:43:24.020 --> 00:43:26.423
They can no longer make
their own proteins.

00:43:27.390 --> 00:43:30.680
The virus proteins plug
up those ribosomes,

00:43:30.680 --> 00:43:34.290
and they only let virus genes in.

00:43:34.290 --> 00:43:36.893
Virus RNA can go in to the ribosomes.

00:43:37.920 --> 00:43:42.274
So it's like the worst
bouncer at the club door.

00:43:42.274 --> 00:43:44.510
It's not gonna let in

00:43:45.449 --> 00:43:48.423
RRNA, it's only gonna
let in the viral RNA.

00:43:50.370 --> 00:43:54.190
And so in other words, the cell's purpose,

00:43:54.190 --> 00:43:56.120
the cell's function has changed,

00:43:56.120 --> 00:43:57.510
and he calls it a virocell.

00:43:57.510 --> 00:44:01.743
And in that regard, Forterre
says viruses are alive.

00:44:03.730 --> 00:44:06.073
And it's easy to sort of to,

00:44:09.900 --> 00:44:13.020
to miss just how much a
virus changes the cell

00:44:13.020 --> 00:44:14.000
once it gets inside,

00:44:14.000 --> 00:44:17.290
so I mentioned it
plugging up the ribosomes,

00:44:17.290 --> 00:44:19.930
here the ribosomes are shown in purple

00:44:19.930 --> 00:44:22.563
in this computer image
of an infected cell,

00:44:24.160 --> 00:44:26.770
but these yellow things,

00:44:26.770 --> 00:44:29.440
these actually represent bubbles,

00:44:29.440 --> 00:44:33.463
that the virus creates
inside of an infected cell.

00:44:34.970 --> 00:44:36.910
So the cell is full of these bubbles,

00:44:36.910 --> 00:44:41.910
and inside of these bubbles
gets, put new viral RNA.

00:44:43.430 --> 00:44:46.163
In other words, genetic
material for new viruses.

00:44:47.136 --> 00:44:50.963
It builds up inside of these
little nurseries, and then,

00:44:51.920 --> 00:44:54.120
it gets more later,
could then get packaged

00:44:54.120 --> 00:44:55.593
into new viruses to leave.

00:44:56.630 --> 00:45:00.410
So again, like if you look
inside of an infected cell

00:45:00.410 --> 00:45:03.740
of virocell, it just doesn't
look like an ordinary cell.

00:45:03.740 --> 00:45:05.690
It looks like a different kind of life.

00:45:07.250 --> 00:45:09.616
And so Forterre who has a
neck for the product says,

00:45:09.616 --> 00:45:14.616
whereas the dream of a normal
cell is to produce more cells

00:45:15.970 --> 00:45:18.080
the dream of a virus is to produce

00:45:18.080 --> 00:45:20.023
a hundred or more new virocells.

00:45:23.490 --> 00:45:27.250
Now, I don't know when
this debate's gonna end,

00:45:27.250 --> 00:45:32.050
and I'm not sure maybe it
can end in the way that

00:45:32.050 --> 00:45:34.403
people are conducting it.

00:45:35.470 --> 00:45:37.923
And, to sort of explain what I mean,

00:45:37.923 --> 00:45:40.200
And, to sort of explain what I mean,

00:45:40.200 --> 00:45:44.390
I wanna just jump back again to the 1930s

00:45:44.390 --> 00:45:47.590
back when viruses were
a deep, deep mystery.

00:45:47.590 --> 00:45:51.410
Norman Pirie, who was
a British virologist,

00:45:51.410 --> 00:45:55.240
who did a lot of important
work on tobacco mosaic virus

00:45:55.240 --> 00:45:56.363
and other viruses,

00:46:00.530 --> 00:46:02.240
he wrote about this exact same question.

00:46:02.240 --> 00:46:05.250
He said, "When one is asked
whether a filter-passing virus

00:46:05.250 --> 00:46:09.210
is living or dead, the
only sensible answer is,

00:46:09.210 --> 00:46:10.960
I don't know."

00:46:10.960 --> 00:46:12.570
We know a number of things they will do,

00:46:12.570 --> 00:46:14.500
and there were things that it won't

00:46:14.500 --> 00:46:17.550
and if someone will
define the word living,

00:46:17.550 --> 00:46:20.573
I will try to see how the
virus fits into the definition.

00:46:21.460 --> 00:46:23.543
So Pirie sort of recognizes that,

00:46:26.550 --> 00:46:31.530
we are trying to pretend
that there's a scientific,

00:46:31.530 --> 00:46:33.780
a clear scientific
question to be addressed

00:46:34.730 --> 00:46:37.750
without recognizing that the
language that we're using

00:46:37.750 --> 00:46:40.350
to frame the question is actually really

00:46:41.220 --> 00:46:42.633
problematic in some ways.

00:46:45.196 --> 00:46:48.723
If someone will define what
living means to me, well, gee,

00:46:49.760 --> 00:46:51.940
I gave you the NASA definition of life.

00:46:51.940 --> 00:46:53.700
I gave you the Merriam Webster definition,

00:46:53.700 --> 00:46:56.543
but there are lots of other definitions.

00:46:57.677 --> 00:47:02.093
These are actually like taken
from the scientific literature

00:47:02.093 --> 00:47:06.740
and these are just a tiny
sampling, there are hundreds,

00:47:06.740 --> 00:47:11.210
several hundred published
definitions of life,

00:47:11.210 --> 00:47:14.763
different ones, and more
get published every year.

00:47:16.254 --> 00:47:19.930
So, if Norman Pirie
was waiting for someone

00:47:19.930 --> 00:47:22.870
to give him a very
clear definition of life

00:47:22.870 --> 00:47:24.500
that he could work
with, just to figure out

00:47:24.500 --> 00:47:25.933
if viruses are alive or not,

00:47:27.000 --> 00:47:30.424
well, 80,90 years later,
we're still waiting

00:47:30.424 --> 00:47:32.400
well, 80,90 years later,
we're still waiting

00:47:32.400 --> 00:47:35.123
for that kind of clarity.

00:47:36.650 --> 00:47:40.630
And maybe that this buzzing
confusion of definitions

00:47:40.630 --> 00:47:41.763
tells us something.

00:47:42.950 --> 00:47:44.880
That's the argument that Carol Cleland,

00:47:44.880 --> 00:47:48.560
a philosopher at the University
of Colorado has made,

00:47:48.560 --> 00:47:51.530
and she wrote a book a couple years ago

00:47:51.530 --> 00:47:53.530
called the quest for
universal theory of life,

00:47:53.530 --> 00:47:54.763
where she lays this out.

00:47:55.700 --> 00:47:58.350
She lays it out as a philosopher.

00:47:58.350 --> 00:48:02.123
And the key point that she makes is that,

00:48:03.760 --> 00:48:08.460
we should not be searching
for a definition of life.

00:48:08.460 --> 00:48:12.380
In fact, definitions
of life get in the way

00:48:13.230 --> 00:48:14.793
of our understanding of life.

00:48:15.680 --> 00:48:18.653
And that might seem weird,
I mean, how can you,

00:48:20.440 --> 00:48:23.200
understand, study something
that you can't even define?

00:48:23.200 --> 00:48:27.423
That sounds like mystical,
but it's actually not.

00:48:28.490 --> 00:48:32.163
The problem is that, definitions,

00:48:33.500 --> 00:48:37.750
philosophically speaking, are
ways of organizing concepts

00:48:37.750 --> 00:48:40.860
and Cleland points out that, let's say,

00:48:40.860 --> 00:48:41.750
what is a bachelor?

00:48:41.750 --> 00:48:43.293
You can define a bachelor,

00:48:44.610 --> 00:48:46.990
because all you're
doing is you're relating

00:48:46.990 --> 00:48:48.740
different concepts to each other.

00:48:48.740 --> 00:48:52.193
So a bachelor is an unmarried man.

00:48:53.690 --> 00:48:55.190
Well, what does it mean to be,

00:48:56.390 --> 00:48:58.070
there's no question about that.

00:48:58.070 --> 00:48:59.250
Now, what is marriage?

00:48:59.250 --> 00:49:02.100
Well, that's complicated.

00:49:02.100 --> 00:49:03.090
What is a man?

00:49:03.090 --> 00:49:04.960
That's complicated too.

00:49:04.960 --> 00:49:09.000
And so there are things
that can be easily defined,

00:49:09.000 --> 00:49:12.140
And so there are things
that can be easily defined,

00:49:12.140 --> 00:49:16.263
cleanly defined and things
that are much more problematic.

00:49:17.330 --> 00:49:22.330
And if you focus on trying
to find just the perfect

00:49:22.590 --> 00:49:26.100
definition, you may not
be spending the time.

00:49:26.100 --> 00:49:28.010
You need to really understand what it is

00:49:28.010 --> 00:49:29.260
that you're dealing with.

00:49:30.590 --> 00:49:34.830
So an example that she gives is water.

00:49:34.830 --> 00:49:35.663
Okay?

00:49:37.453 --> 00:49:38.286
So what is water?

00:49:38.286 --> 00:49:39.430
How do you define water?

00:49:39.430 --> 00:49:43.027
Well, 500 years ago if you went
and talked with alchemists,

00:49:43.027 --> 00:49:46.170
they were the ones who
understood what we call

00:49:46.170 --> 00:49:49.150
chemistry best, and they
would define it for you

00:49:49.150 --> 00:49:52.240
as being transparent, as being liquid,

00:49:52.240 --> 00:49:54.310
as being able to dissolve certain things,

00:49:54.310 --> 00:49:56.451
they'd give you a list of characteristics.

00:49:56.451 --> 00:49:59.140
But that very problematic, because

00:50:01.310 --> 00:50:03.850
for example what happens
when water gets cold

00:50:03.850 --> 00:50:07.270
and suddenly you have
a solid, is ice water?

00:50:07.270 --> 00:50:09.923
Well, no, not according to the definition.

00:50:11.230 --> 00:50:13.310
Then you would have
these different kinds of

00:50:13.310 --> 00:50:17.150
transparent liquids, like
could dissolve things,

00:50:17.150 --> 00:50:18.770
but some could dissolve some things

00:50:18.770 --> 00:50:20.170
and some could solve other things.

00:50:20.170 --> 00:50:22.303
So are they all water?

00:50:23.400 --> 00:50:25.610
Alchemists would try to deal with this

00:50:25.610 --> 00:50:28.490
by calling some things
Royal water, for example.

00:50:28.490 --> 00:50:30.943
Which we would know to be
certain kinds of acids.

00:50:32.700 --> 00:50:35.530
Even Leonard Da Vinci at the time

00:50:35.530 --> 00:50:37.860
was just left mystified by water.

00:50:37.860 --> 00:50:39.980
So you would talk about how
water was sometimes weak

00:50:39.980 --> 00:50:43.310
and sometimes strong, sometimes
bitter, sometimes sweet,

00:50:43.310 --> 00:50:45.170
sometimes thick or thin,

00:50:45.170 --> 00:50:47.050
sometimes bringing hurt or pestilence,

00:50:47.050 --> 00:50:48.283
sometimes health giving.

00:50:49.261 --> 00:50:51.990
So it suffers changes in as many natures

00:50:51.990 --> 00:50:54.633
as are the different places
through which it passes.

00:50:58.278 --> 00:51:00.240
So Leonard Da Vinci would not have gotten

00:51:00.240 --> 00:51:03.010
any greater clarity, just
trying to come up with

00:51:03.010 --> 00:51:04.343
a definition of life.

00:51:05.790 --> 00:51:09.600
What was needed was 300 years of research

00:51:09.600 --> 00:51:14.320
of experiments of thinking and theorizing,

00:51:15.590 --> 00:51:18.670
and coming up with a theory of matter

00:51:18.670 --> 00:51:20.320
in which you could explain water.

00:51:21.470 --> 00:51:24.810
So once you have the language of atoms,

00:51:24.810 --> 00:51:27.230
molecules, elements, and so on,

00:51:27.230 --> 00:51:32.230
you can think about water in terms of H2O.

00:51:32.300 --> 00:51:36.770
And you can think about water
as being molecules in motion.

00:51:36.770 --> 00:51:39.490
and at certain temperatures
that becomes ice.

00:51:39.490 --> 00:51:42.053
So that yes, ice is water,
sure of course it is.

00:51:43.420 --> 00:51:45.095
And it's not so much that you're seeking

00:51:45.095 --> 00:51:48.460
And it's not so much that you're seeking

00:51:48.460 --> 00:51:51.020
to define water now, you're
just trying to understand it.

00:51:51.020 --> 00:51:54.160
You're trying to see how water
behaves in different ways

00:51:54.160 --> 00:51:56.290
and make predictions about it and so on

00:51:56.290 --> 00:51:58.580
and use it in interesting ways

00:51:58.580 --> 00:52:00.180
that you hadn't expected before.

00:52:01.840 --> 00:52:04.507
So this is the difference
between a definition of life

00:52:04.507 --> 00:52:05.883
and a theory of life.

00:52:07.670 --> 00:52:10.360
We don't have a theory of life,
but what would it be like?

00:52:10.360 --> 00:52:15.343
Well, it would be a way
to explain how matter

00:52:16.620 --> 00:52:20.680
takes on certain properties and we could

00:52:20.680 --> 00:52:22.290
debate what those properties are,

00:52:22.290 --> 00:52:25.910
but what I like is the
analogy of super conductivity

00:52:27.560 --> 00:52:29.780
over a century ago, scientists discovered

00:52:29.780 --> 00:52:32.150
super conductivity, it was very weird,

00:52:32.150 --> 00:52:36.710
there were these substances
that lost their resistance

00:52:36.710 --> 00:52:38.200
to electric current.

00:52:38.200 --> 00:52:40.900
When you got them very
cold, didn't make sense.

00:52:40.900 --> 00:52:42.430
Einstein couldn't explain it,

00:52:42.430 --> 00:52:44.260
Richard Feynman couldn't explain it,

00:52:44.260 --> 00:52:47.200
but eventually some folks got together

00:52:47.200 --> 00:52:49.000
and made a theory of superconductivity.

00:52:49.000 --> 00:52:53.300
A theory that explained it in
terms of pairs of Electrons

00:52:53.300 --> 00:52:54.490
And now it made sense.

00:52:54.490 --> 00:52:56.570
Now you could see how different substances

00:52:56.570 --> 00:52:59.332
could actually be superconductive,

00:52:59.332 --> 00:53:02.800
and that then allowed
people to start making

00:53:02.800 --> 00:53:05.490
new kinds of superconductive material.

00:53:05.490 --> 00:53:07.923
So, and you could start making things

00:53:07.923 --> 00:53:10.920
that could support trains, for example.

00:53:10.920 --> 00:53:15.413
So it's a theory that we
need, a theory perhaps about,

00:53:16.290 --> 00:53:18.920
organization and information.

00:53:18.920 --> 00:53:21.920
Maybe that's something that really is

00:53:21.920 --> 00:53:23.863
what needs to be explained for life.

00:53:25.500 --> 00:53:27.508
Just to give you one example,

00:53:27.508 --> 00:53:30.340
there's a theory called Assembly theory

00:53:30.340 --> 00:53:33.310
that Lee Cronin at the
University of Glasgow

00:53:33.310 --> 00:53:35.460
has been exploring.

00:53:35.460 --> 00:53:39.143
So it's not about the
specifics of DNA and protein,

00:53:40.040 --> 00:53:42.310
but maybe just about
the steps that it takes

00:53:42.310 --> 00:53:45.230
to make molecules.

00:53:45.230 --> 00:53:49.250
So maybe what's distinctive
about life is that

00:53:49.250 --> 00:53:53.140
life and only life can
make certain molecules

00:53:53.140 --> 00:53:56.253
that require lots and
lots of steps of assembly.

00:53:57.450 --> 00:54:00.740
And that it's a simple idea,

00:54:00.740 --> 00:54:03.960
but it's actually a potentially
a very powerful one,

00:54:03.960 --> 00:54:07.610
because, maybe you don't
need to know in advance

00:54:07.610 --> 00:54:10.940
what living things are
made of on other planets.

00:54:10.940 --> 00:54:13.360
You don't need to be just
looking for DNA based life.

00:54:13.360 --> 00:54:18.110
Maybe there's on a moon like
Enceladus over at Saturn,

00:54:18.110 --> 00:54:20.020
maybe life is totally different.

00:54:20.020 --> 00:54:22.570
Maybe assembly theory can
help us to understand it.

00:54:24.200 --> 00:54:28.080
And it's possible that maybe life isn't

00:54:28.080 --> 00:54:29.970
going to be discovered on another planet.

00:54:29.970 --> 00:54:32.983
Maybe, someone in a lab was gonna make it.

00:54:34.100 --> 00:54:37.220
These are little droplets
of mixtures of oil

00:54:37.220 --> 00:54:39.350
that actually Lee Cronin
and his colleagues

00:54:39.350 --> 00:54:40.630
have have produced

00:54:41.810 --> 00:54:44.930
Just mixing different things
around you get droplets

00:54:44.930 --> 00:54:46.593
that do weird stuff.

00:54:47.550 --> 00:54:50.810
He refers to them as unstable proto cells.

00:54:50.810 --> 00:54:55.810
They move around, they divide,
they do lifelike things.

00:54:56.640 --> 00:55:00.150
They're very strange, I don't
know, we quite call them life,

00:55:00.150 --> 00:55:04.190
but they might be telling
us something about

00:55:04.190 --> 00:55:08.260
how living things first arose.

00:55:08.260 --> 00:55:13.260
And we could theories to
understand what we're looking at.

00:55:16.380 --> 00:55:18.850
But this really takes us back 250 years

00:55:18.850 --> 00:55:22.400
because when I think when we're looking at

00:55:22.400 --> 00:55:23.860
these amazing experiments,

00:55:23.860 --> 00:55:25.900
like what's happening in this dish,

00:55:25.900 --> 00:55:28.590
I think we're kind of like Goeze was,

00:55:28.590 --> 00:55:30.010
where he talks about a glass lens,

00:55:30.010 --> 00:55:32.280
that shows us a new world.

00:55:32.280 --> 00:55:37.280
I think that we in a
sense are looking at life

00:55:37.710 --> 00:55:40.800
for the first time, with
this new perspective.

00:55:40.800 --> 00:55:45.650
And we're just beginning to
maybe to be understanding it

00:55:45.650 --> 00:55:47.533
in this new and profound way.

00:55:49.390 --> 00:55:52.712
So thank you so much for your time

00:55:52.712 --> 00:55:56.030
there's lots more about these issues

00:55:56.030 --> 00:56:00.153
in my book "Life's Edge"
and I'm more than happy

00:56:00.153 --> 00:56:03.303
to take questions if people have them.

00:56:05.060 --> 00:56:08.490
- Thank you so much Carl
for that wonderful talk,

00:56:08.490 --> 00:56:13.490
outlining the complexities
of the semantic definition

00:56:14.200 --> 00:56:19.180
of what life is because of,
the biological, chemical

00:56:19.180 --> 00:56:24.180
and other properties that
we attribute to life forms.

00:56:24.330 --> 00:56:27.140
And this is a question
that's of great importance,

00:56:27.140 --> 00:56:29.260
even in the space sciences at the moment

00:56:29.260 --> 00:56:31.350
where people are trying to understand

00:56:31.350 --> 00:56:36.350
if we do discover signs
of extraterrestrial life,

00:56:36.860 --> 00:56:39.430
what might that look like?

00:56:39.430 --> 00:56:41.630
And then of course, we
may have to even expand

00:56:41.630 --> 00:56:46.340
beyond the idea of carbon based
life which is where we are,

00:56:46.340 --> 00:56:49.040
fighting over the definitions on earth.

00:56:49.040 --> 00:56:51.030
Thank you so much for that wonderful talk,

00:56:51.030 --> 00:56:54.290
so let's give Carl a clap, a virtual clap

00:56:54.290 --> 00:56:57.570
so let's give Carl a clap, a virtual clap

00:56:57.570 --> 00:57:00.450
before we open up for questions.

00:57:00.450 --> 00:57:02.960
So I think we have a few questions

00:57:02.960 --> 00:57:05.203
that have come in to the chat.

00:57:07.520 --> 00:57:10.093
The first one is from Seana,

00:57:11.100 --> 00:57:13.000
who would like to hear your thoughts

00:57:13.000 --> 00:57:16.060
on the theory of symbiogenesis,

00:57:16.060 --> 00:57:19.250
taking more of a center
stage in common knowledge

00:57:19.250 --> 00:57:22.470
and the future role of quantum mechanics

00:57:22.470 --> 00:57:24.933
in explaining question in biology.

00:57:27.020 --> 00:57:28.930
- Wow, well a great question.

00:57:28.930 --> 00:57:33.090
Yeah, so symbiogenesis is this
idea of different organisms

00:57:33.090 --> 00:57:38.090
Yeah, so symbiogenesis is this
idea of different organisms

00:57:38.220 --> 00:57:41.930
or different living systems
coming together in a symbiosis

00:57:41.930 --> 00:57:45.690
to create new levels of life?

00:57:45.690 --> 00:57:50.360
So for example, the best example would be

00:57:50.360 --> 00:57:55.310
in our cells we produce
fuel in mitochondria,

00:57:55.310 --> 00:58:00.310
and those mitochondria, basically
are little house guests,

00:58:02.600 --> 00:58:06.480
they're bacteria that
took up residents inside

00:58:06.480 --> 00:58:10.160
of our single celled ancestors

00:58:10.160 --> 00:58:12.650
maybe I guess about 2 billion years ago,

00:58:12.650 --> 00:58:15.503
something like that, and,

00:58:20.600 --> 00:58:23.510
there was a mutual benefit
there in the sense that

00:58:23.510 --> 00:58:27.270
the bacteria were getting shelter

00:58:27.270 --> 00:58:29.962
and they were getting some
provisions and then they could,

00:58:29.962 --> 00:58:34.494
actually produce things like
ATP fuel for the host cell.

00:58:34.494 --> 00:58:37.360
actually produce things like
ATP fuel for the host cell.

00:58:37.360 --> 00:58:41.860
And eventually it became
impossible to distinguish

00:58:41.860 --> 00:58:46.860
the two of them there became
just one greater unit.

00:58:48.300 --> 00:58:53.300
And when people like Lynn
Margulis were arguing for this

00:58:53.350 --> 00:58:56.880
in the sixties, it was
fairly controversial,

00:58:56.880 --> 00:58:59.283
it wasn't clear how important it was, but,

00:59:00.460 --> 00:59:03.533
along with mitochondria, there
are lots of other examples,

00:59:04.450 --> 00:59:07.143
whether you're talking
about the chloroplasts

00:59:07.143 --> 00:59:11.500
that make plants green or actually insects

00:59:13.817 --> 00:59:18.620
insects and other invertebrates
have lots of bacteria

00:59:18.620 --> 00:59:22.490
inside of them, the bacteria
actually inside of their cells

00:59:22.490 --> 00:59:26.810
and can only live there and
they have this remarkable

00:59:28.210 --> 00:59:31.500
symbiosis, so like cicadas for example,

00:59:31.500 --> 00:59:35.083
which we have these
emergence of cicadas and,

00:59:36.200 --> 00:59:41.200
the cicadas are eating fluid
from trees and there's not much

00:59:41.790 --> 00:59:44.710
nutrient in it and on its own

00:59:44.710 --> 00:59:47.280
the Cicada couldn't survive on it.

00:59:47.280 --> 00:59:49.850
But you feed that to the
bacteria that it harbors

00:59:49.850 --> 00:59:53.410
and the bacteria can actually
build essential amino acids

00:59:53.410 --> 00:59:55.673
and then give them to the insect.

00:59:56.690 --> 01:00:00.383
And so you have a symbiogenesis
right there as well.

01:00:01.660 --> 01:00:05.250
I don't think that symbiogenesis
will necessarily tell us

01:00:05.250 --> 01:00:08.060
that much about the very origin of life,

01:00:08.060 --> 01:00:11.200
just because you need
those complicated things

01:00:11.200 --> 01:00:14.050
to begin with, to have
them to come together,

01:00:14.050 --> 01:00:16.660
bacteria already pretty complicated.

01:00:16.660 --> 01:00:19.090
But it is possible to think about,

01:00:19.090 --> 01:00:21.083
people think on the early earth,

01:00:23.100 --> 01:00:27.570
the earliest life forms might
have had RNA inside of them,

01:00:27.570 --> 01:00:32.210
or maybe just RNA floating
around and sort of oily layers

01:00:32.210 --> 01:00:35.140
at the bottom of ponds
or something like that.

01:00:35.140 --> 01:00:37.040
So you could have different RNA molecules

01:00:37.040 --> 01:00:38.630
cooperating with each other

01:00:38.630 --> 01:00:42.720
in a kind of molecular
symbiosis that's possible.

01:00:42.720 --> 01:00:43.920
As for quantum mechanics

01:00:45.745 --> 01:00:47.970
I mean obviously like when you get down

01:00:47.970 --> 01:00:50.420
to the level of atoms,
quantum mechanics matters,

01:00:53.236 --> 01:00:56.243
I don't know, I mean when I
started as a science writer,

01:00:56.243 --> 01:00:58.870
over 30 years ago,
people were talking about

01:00:58.870 --> 01:01:00.460
the revolution of quantum mechanics

01:01:00.460 --> 01:01:03.183
to change the way we
think about biology and,

01:01:06.020 --> 01:01:08.340
it's important, if you're
understanding like vision

01:01:08.340 --> 01:01:11.520
and for example, in levels
of energy levels of photons

01:01:11.520 --> 01:01:16.520
and so on but I don't think
that, I haven't seen much yet

01:01:17.860 --> 01:01:20.290
in how quantum mechanics really changes

01:01:20.290 --> 01:01:23.950
the way we think about
important kinds of biology,

01:01:23.950 --> 01:01:26.870
or principles of evolution, but we'll see

01:01:26.870 --> 01:01:28.540
the guy could change.

01:01:28.540 --> 01:01:31.310
- So somewhat related to
that Carl, I had a question.

01:01:31.310 --> 01:01:35.440
So I mean, in our view
of evolution, right?

01:01:35.440 --> 01:01:39.400
It's very much driven by
this idea of competition,

01:01:39.400 --> 01:01:41.050
and so as you just mentioned, right?

01:01:41.050 --> 01:01:44.270
So isn't there and sort of an
emerging understanding now,

01:01:44.270 --> 01:01:47.080
on competition and cooperation,

01:01:47.080 --> 01:01:51.363
both being elements of how
evolution actually operates?

01:01:52.640 --> 01:01:56.346
- Sure and Darwin was
intrigued by cooperation

01:01:56.346 --> 01:01:59.180
- Sure and Darwin was
intrigued by cooperation

01:01:59.180 --> 01:02:02.710
from the very start of evolutionary theory

01:02:02.710 --> 01:02:05.260
and I mean he certainly was aware of

01:02:05.260 --> 01:02:07.513
they have the importance of cooperation.

01:02:09.150 --> 01:02:10.413
I think that,

01:02:11.450 --> 01:02:16.450
I mean, basically, there are
situations in which cooperation

01:02:17.420 --> 01:02:22.420
is going to be the most
advantageous evolutionary strategy,

01:02:22.900 --> 01:02:24.620
and there are gonna be situations

01:02:24.620 --> 01:02:26.763
and for certain organisms where it's not.

01:02:28.670 --> 01:02:29.503
So,

01:02:33.085 --> 01:02:36.940
when SARS-CoV-2 is
infecting us for example,

01:02:36.940 --> 01:02:41.840
and you get mutant variance
popping up inside your body,

01:02:41.840 --> 01:02:45.350
there is a competition going
on among those viruses.

01:02:45.350 --> 01:02:47.846
It's sort of unconscious competition.

01:02:47.846 --> 01:02:50.070
It's not like a race or something.

01:02:50.070 --> 01:02:52.233
It's just like the question is, well,

01:02:54.280 --> 01:02:57.050
the viruses with different mutations,

01:02:57.050 --> 01:02:59.503
which ones will make the most viruses?

01:03:03.500 --> 01:03:05.173
And then over the long term,

01:03:06.183 --> 01:03:09.830
the mutations that increase
the fitness of viruses the most

01:03:09.830 --> 01:03:11.730
may become the ones that are dominant.

01:03:13.770 --> 01:03:16.810
On the other hand, the
fact is that viruses,

01:03:16.810 --> 01:03:20.370
this is kind of weird, but
viruses do sometimes cooperate.

01:03:20.370 --> 01:03:25.370
They actually work together
to different kinds of viruses

01:03:25.410 --> 01:03:27.270
will get into the same
cell at the same time

01:03:27.270 --> 01:03:31.370
and manipulate it together
using their pooled resources,

01:03:31.370 --> 01:03:34.340
to make more of each kind of virus.

01:03:34.340 --> 01:03:39.340
So, that's, you're still getting sick.

01:03:39.654 --> 01:03:43.180
So that cooperation isn't
very warm and fuzzy for you,

01:03:43.180 --> 01:03:46.190
the sick host, but, it's
pretty cool that the viruses

01:03:46.190 --> 01:03:47.830
are cooperating as well.

01:03:47.830 --> 01:03:52.110
So they're both definitely
important for evolution.

01:03:52.110 --> 01:03:53.338
- Great, thank you.

01:03:53.338 --> 01:03:56.540
So we have a question
now from Deepti Pradhan

01:03:56.540 --> 01:03:59.860
who writes the famous Tilde Science Café,

01:03:59.860 --> 01:04:02.570
and her question is where
would one play viruses

01:04:02.570 --> 01:04:03.723
on the tree of life?

01:04:05.100 --> 01:04:10.100
- Great question, yeah, so the
tree of life was this image

01:04:10.160 --> 01:04:14.140
that Darwin invoked in
the origin of species,

01:04:14.140 --> 01:04:19.140
because he saw species as
sharing a common ancestor,

01:04:20.120 --> 01:04:23.580
involving splitting all
off from a common ancestor,

01:04:23.580 --> 01:04:25.383
like branches on a tree.

01:04:26.840 --> 01:04:31.550
And so we share an ancestor
that lived maybe six

01:04:31.550 --> 01:04:34.400
or 7 million years ago with chimpanzees.

01:04:34.400 --> 01:04:36.020
We share an ancestor that maybe lived

01:04:36.020 --> 01:04:38.210
10 million years ago with gorillas.

01:04:38.210 --> 01:04:41.640
Those that are our
closest living relatives

01:04:41.640 --> 01:04:45.050
and for animals like you
can make a whole tree

01:04:45.050 --> 01:04:47.932
like showing how animals are related

01:04:47.932 --> 01:04:49.730
and descend from a common ancestor.

01:04:49.730 --> 01:04:53.610
There was a primal animal,
from whom we all descend.

01:04:53.610 --> 01:04:55.620
And that's all animals,
that's tardigrades,

01:04:55.620 --> 01:04:59.310
and jellyfish and sharks, all of us.

01:04:59.310 --> 01:05:00.760
And if you step back further,

01:05:02.160 --> 01:05:05.453
we share a ancestor with fungi,

01:05:06.350 --> 01:05:07.910
and if you step back even further,

01:05:07.910 --> 01:05:09.920
we share a common a we share with plants.

01:05:09.920 --> 01:05:12.900
So we're closer cousins
to fungi than plants.

01:05:12.900 --> 01:05:15.573
It's something that's
worth bearing in mind,

01:05:16.950 --> 01:05:20.100
step back even further, and
you can start to see how

01:05:20.100 --> 01:05:23.300
we're related to protozoans
and then to bacteria.

01:05:23.300 --> 01:05:26.283
So all cellular life can pretty,

01:05:27.170 --> 01:05:31.090
pretty well be organized
on a tree of life,

01:05:31.090 --> 01:05:33.190
looking at their genes and comparing them.

01:05:34.287 --> 01:05:35.820
Then you got viruses.

01:05:35.820 --> 01:05:37.483
What do you do with these things?

01:05:38.770 --> 01:05:43.170
How are they related to if
at all, to anything else?

01:05:43.170 --> 01:05:46.270
The part of the problem is
that viruses are so small

01:05:46.270 --> 01:05:49.900
and they have such tiny genomes

01:05:49.900 --> 01:05:52.685
that there's just not
that much information

01:05:52.685 --> 01:05:55.590
to link them to anything.

01:05:55.590 --> 01:05:56.950
So it's very hard to know,

01:05:56.950 --> 01:05:59.940
I mean you can tell how viruses
are related to each other,

01:05:59.940 --> 01:06:02.500
but it can be very
challenging to link them to

01:06:02.500 --> 01:06:03.600
the rest of life.

01:06:03.600 --> 01:06:06.206
But there are some clues as to how,

01:06:06.206 --> 01:06:09.253
But there are some clues as to how,

01:06:10.470 --> 01:06:15.470
genetic material from cellular
life may be able to break off

01:06:16.560 --> 01:06:18.403
and become viral.

01:06:20.210 --> 01:06:22.190
And in fact,

01:06:23.400 --> 01:06:25.250
it goes the other way as well, that,

01:06:25.250 --> 01:06:27.120
we have been infected by viruses,

01:06:27.120 --> 01:06:28.920
our ancestors for millions of years.

01:06:29.940 --> 01:06:32.130
Some of that viral DNA
has been incorporated

01:06:32.130 --> 01:06:33.820
into our genome.

01:06:33.820 --> 01:06:38.593
So we're about 8% of our
DNA comes from viruses.

01:06:39.520 --> 01:06:40.533
So,

01:06:42.250 --> 01:06:44.120
it's possible viruses where there

01:06:44.120 --> 01:06:46.110
at the very beginning of life,

01:06:46.110 --> 01:06:49.260
maybe viruses started
out as parasitic RNA.

01:06:49.260 --> 01:06:50.093
We don't know.

01:06:55.318 --> 01:06:57.257
So viruses may sort of like
hover around the tree of life.

01:06:59.210 --> 01:07:01.510
Maybe that's the best
way of thinking of them.

01:07:02.770 --> 01:07:06.983
- Thank you, I think you've definitely,

01:07:07.940 --> 01:07:10.290
shown us the paradoxes

01:07:10.290 --> 01:07:14.750
of how we could deal with the viruses.

01:07:14.750 --> 01:07:16.770
So the next question is from Agatha,

01:07:16.770 --> 01:07:19.580
which of the definitions of life resonates

01:07:19.580 --> 01:07:21.853
most closely with you and why?

01:07:25.530 --> 01:07:28.200
- Yeah, I know, like it
sort of depends on the day

01:07:35.610 --> 01:07:39.160
I write about the creator
of one of those definitions

01:07:39.160 --> 01:07:42.610
in the book named Albert Szent-Györgyi.

01:07:43.460 --> 01:07:45.620
He was a Hungarian Nobel prize winner.

01:07:45.620 --> 01:07:49.323
He won the Nobel prize for
discovering vitamin C basically.

01:07:50.310 --> 01:07:54.370
And once he had won the
Nobel prize for vitamin C,

01:07:54.370 --> 01:07:56.040
then he decided, well, okay,

01:07:56.040 --> 01:07:59.270
now I can get down to
some really serious work.

01:07:59.270 --> 01:08:01.940
And so he really wanted
to get down to the very

01:08:01.940 --> 01:08:05.120
nuts and bolts of life,
really like life itself

01:08:05.120 --> 01:08:09.320
on a sort of biophysical level.

01:08:09.320 --> 01:08:12.201
And he did amazing work.

01:08:12.201 --> 01:08:16.623
He actually got, he was in Hungary and,

01:08:18.420 --> 01:08:19.270
and was starting this research,

01:08:19.270 --> 01:08:22.123
right when world war II breaks out.

01:08:23.830 --> 01:08:27.890
He becomes a spy, he joins resistance,

01:08:27.890 --> 01:08:32.290
and he's on the run around Hungary,

01:08:32.290 --> 01:08:34.590
but he would show up every
now and then unexpectedly

01:08:34.590 --> 01:08:38.640
in his lab, to make sure the
experiments are going okay.

01:08:38.640 --> 01:08:42.050
Where they're trying to understand
how muscles fiber works.

01:08:42.050 --> 01:08:45.053
'Cause he really feels that
that's gonna give the key.

01:08:46.310 --> 01:08:49.880
And then, he actually
then smuggles it out.

01:08:49.880 --> 01:08:52.560
He gets a Swedish diplomat to smuggle out

01:08:53.460 --> 01:08:56.590
the manuscript about
these findings because

01:08:56.590 --> 01:08:58.330
he's terrified that no one's gonna know

01:08:58.330 --> 01:08:59.430
about what they found.

01:09:00.520 --> 01:09:02.700
And a lot of people think
maybe he should have gotten

01:09:02.700 --> 01:09:04.450
a second Nobel prize for that, but,

01:09:07.444 --> 01:09:10.490
he joked afterwards after that work

01:09:10.490 --> 01:09:13.620
and after the war that he was
working so hard to figure out

01:09:13.620 --> 01:09:17.763
what life was but it was just
sort of, it kept escaping him.

01:09:20.699 --> 01:09:23.195
And after a while, he was just like, well,

01:09:23.195 --> 01:09:27.830
I'd like to call life a play of water.

01:09:27.830 --> 01:09:30.640
Because he was interested
in the way in which,

01:09:30.640 --> 01:09:34.130
a lot of the key reactions,

01:09:34.130 --> 01:09:36.660
that happened in his cell
involved water molecules,

01:09:36.660 --> 01:09:41.320
being broken up and being used
to carry out these reactions.

01:09:41.320 --> 01:09:44.520
So he just said, life
is just a play of water.

01:09:44.520 --> 01:09:46.003
So I can live with that.

01:09:48.470 --> 01:09:51.360
- So the other question,

01:09:51.360 --> 01:09:53.970
the next question is even from Mada Suden,

01:09:53.970 --> 01:09:57.090
who says, even
self-sustaining living systems

01:09:57.090 --> 01:09:58.950
cannot be a closed system.

01:09:58.950 --> 01:10:01.670
They need external sources of energy.

01:10:01.670 --> 01:10:05.370
If you put things in that way,
is a virus that different?

01:10:05.370 --> 01:10:08.813
They need external matter to
exhibit other signs of life.

01:10:09.790 --> 01:10:11.620
And they're asking for your comment.

01:10:11.620 --> 01:10:14.020
- Yeah, I know, that's a
very interesting point.

01:10:16.810 --> 01:10:18.410
I think in biology, there's been

01:10:19.584 --> 01:10:23.353
a very long running division
between parasites, and,

01:10:27.517 --> 01:10:29.873
"Free living hosts"

01:10:34.128 --> 01:10:35.410
and but you're right, like, I mean,

01:10:35.410 --> 01:10:38.706
who is really like totally
free living Like, I mean,

01:10:38.706 --> 01:10:41.903
we depend on other living things,

01:10:43.737 --> 01:10:45.280
we all carry around a microbiome.

01:10:45.280 --> 01:10:47.763
We'd be very sick without it, and so,

01:10:49.500 --> 01:10:54.500
so I agree that it's, we
can't be too contemptuous

01:10:54.760 --> 01:10:56.140
as there were are viruses,

01:10:56.140 --> 01:11:00.083
but I still think that
the fact remains that,

01:11:01.066 --> 01:11:04.961
viruses are really different than cells

01:11:04.961 --> 01:11:07.043
in some fundamental ways.

01:11:08.840 --> 01:11:11.070
But who knows scientists
are finding some really

01:11:11.070 --> 01:11:14.320
weird viruses these days,
like really big viruses

01:11:14.320 --> 01:11:16.060
with loss and loss of genes.

01:11:16.060 --> 01:11:20.903
And some of those genes seem
to encode for parts of cells,

01:11:21.940 --> 01:11:23.721
like ribosomes or parts of the
apparatus for photosynthesis.

01:11:23.721 --> 01:11:28.630
like ribosomes or parts of the
apparatus for photosynthesis.

01:11:28.630 --> 01:11:31.480
So, there's some weird things

01:11:31.480 --> 01:11:34.280
about viruses, I'm sure will
find viruses that really,

01:11:34.280 --> 01:11:37.043
defy our easy definitions even more.

01:11:40.400 --> 01:11:42.580
- Thank you, thank you.

01:11:42.580 --> 01:11:45.840
So there's a comment from Gary who says,

01:11:45.840 --> 01:11:46.693
What is life?

01:11:46.693 --> 01:11:50.030
The question needs a
context on who is asking

01:11:50.030 --> 01:11:53.650
that in what domain
and the important issue

01:11:53.650 --> 01:11:56.390
is knowing the mechanism
as to what is going on

01:11:56.390 --> 01:12:00.020
in a particular domain and
then you can assign terms

01:12:00.020 --> 01:12:03.790
either new ones or old
ones to different parts

01:12:03.790 --> 01:12:07.823
of the process, to
further experimental work.

01:12:11.120 --> 01:12:16.033
- Well, certainly it helps to,

01:12:17.450 --> 01:12:20.253
it can be useful to have
working definitions of life,

01:12:21.904 --> 01:12:23.487
for particular work,

01:12:23.487 --> 01:12:25.154
for particular work,

01:12:26.620 --> 01:12:27.950
the working definitions.

01:12:27.950 --> 01:12:30.993
So if you wanna go and find,

01:12:32.460 --> 01:12:34.720
alien life and you're gonna spend

01:12:34.720 --> 01:12:36.210
hundreds of billions of dollars

01:12:36.210 --> 01:12:39.690
trying to do it, like it might
be nice to be able to know

01:12:39.690 --> 01:12:42.640
what it is that you're even
thinking about finding.

01:12:42.640 --> 01:12:47.040
But even then I talked
to an astrobiologist

01:12:47.040 --> 01:12:50.580
of who appears in the book,
Laurie Barge, and I said to her,

01:12:50.580 --> 01:12:53.810
so she's in Enceladus
which I shared before

01:12:53.810 --> 01:12:58.060
this moon of Saturn, she'd
really liked to go to Enceladus

01:12:58.060 --> 01:13:01.370
because it's got covered in ice,

01:13:01.370 --> 01:13:03.920
but it clearly has a lot of
weird chemistry happening

01:13:03.920 --> 01:13:05.393
inside of its liquid ocean.

01:13:06.320 --> 01:13:09.910
And I'm like, well, what's
your definition of life?

01:13:09.910 --> 01:13:12.440
Like, what would it have to be

01:13:12.440 --> 01:13:13.650
in order for you to say it's alive?

01:13:13.650 --> 01:13:18.650
And she's like, I prefer not
to deal with things like,

01:13:19.200 --> 01:13:20.600
I don't like to define life.

01:13:21.660 --> 01:13:23.162
This is literally an astrobiologist,

01:13:23.162 --> 01:13:24.530
his job is to find life.

01:13:24.530 --> 01:13:25.363
She's like,

01:13:26.490 --> 01:13:30.300
I'd like to go there and
see what the chemistry is.

01:13:30.300 --> 01:13:32.370
And I bet it's very interesting

01:13:33.580 --> 01:13:36.450
and I'll just wanna
understand what it's doing

01:13:36.450 --> 01:13:37.930
and what makes it interesting.

01:13:37.930 --> 01:13:41.770
And maybe eventually
we might come to agree

01:13:41.770 --> 01:13:43.440
that there's something there we call life.

01:13:43.440 --> 01:13:47.977
But what if it was chemistry
that in 10 million years

01:13:47.977 --> 01:13:52.140
But what if it was chemistry
that in 10 million years

01:13:53.370 --> 01:13:57.640
will give rise to life, like
you wouldn't wanna just dismiss

01:13:57.640 --> 01:13:59.623
it out out of hand before.

01:14:00.776 --> 01:14:02.380
So it's interesting even astrobiologists

01:14:02.380 --> 01:14:04.080
sometimes don't wanna define life.

01:14:06.910 --> 01:14:08.160
- Thank you, Carl.

01:14:08.160 --> 01:14:10.470
The next question is from Brian,

01:14:10.470 --> 01:14:13.540
are there genetically similar
creatures to tardigrades?

01:14:13.540 --> 01:14:17.643
If so, do they display the
cryptobiological process?

01:14:18.890 --> 01:14:21.800
- So tardigrades are
just, they're animals.

01:14:21.800 --> 01:14:22.633
They're,

01:14:24.720 --> 01:14:26.690
I forget what their scientists are always

01:14:26.690 --> 01:14:29.720
trying to figure out how
an closely different parts

01:14:29.720 --> 01:14:31.530
of the animal kingdom are
related to each other.

01:14:31.530 --> 01:14:36.530
So, I think they're kind of
closely related to insects

01:14:36.610 --> 01:14:40.073
as I recall now, but, they're
their own branch, but,

01:14:41.780 --> 01:14:43.970
so they share a common ancestor with,

01:14:43.970 --> 01:14:46.370
with other animals like us

01:14:46.370 --> 01:14:50.943
that have, brains and guts
and nervous systems and so on.

01:14:52.630 --> 01:14:56.512
Now cryptobiosis, what they're able to do

01:14:56.512 --> 01:14:59.193
is actually found in a number of animals,

01:15:00.550 --> 01:15:04.544
lots of little invertebrates that do it,

01:15:04.544 --> 01:15:07.340
and fungi and bacteria

01:15:07.340 --> 01:15:09.600
seem to be doing
something similar as well.

01:15:09.600 --> 01:15:12.333
So what's interesting is that,

01:15:13.280 --> 01:15:15.430
I showed you the tardigrade strategy,

01:15:15.430 --> 01:15:19.030
which is essentially to
like just replace your water

01:15:19.030 --> 01:15:21.730
with sugar and then turn your body

01:15:21.730 --> 01:15:23.470
into basically a protein glass.

01:15:23.470 --> 01:15:25.250
That's one way of doing it.

01:15:25.250 --> 01:15:28.790
Other branches of the
tree of life have evolved

01:15:28.790 --> 01:15:31.920
other strategies to do the same thing.

01:15:31.920 --> 01:15:35.753
But the result is the
same that these things are

01:15:37.350 --> 01:15:39.190
somewhere between life and death,

01:15:39.190 --> 01:15:40.650
and can stay that way for decades

01:15:40.650 --> 01:15:41.750
as far as we can tell.

01:15:44.590 --> 01:15:48.040
- Great, the next question is from Diane.

01:15:48.040 --> 01:15:51.070
So since prions lack genetic material,

01:15:51.070 --> 01:15:53.717
is there consensus that
they're not alive.?

01:15:58.510 --> 01:16:00.440
- Again, it all comes down

01:16:00.440 --> 01:16:03.083
to what your definition of life is.

01:16:04.200 --> 01:16:05.383
And so,

01:16:09.483 --> 01:16:13.593
they are again kind of like
a they're at "Life's Edge"

01:16:15.510 --> 01:16:18.130
they might fulfill some parts of the

01:16:18.130 --> 01:16:21.430
of common definitions
of life and fail others.

01:16:21.430 --> 01:16:26.430
So for example, so prions are
proteins, just naked proteins,

01:16:27.100 --> 01:16:32.100
but they are sort of
deformed versions of proteins

01:16:32.100 --> 01:16:33.583
that we make ourselves.

01:16:34.620 --> 01:16:38.630
And so if a prion version of
the protein comes up next to

01:16:38.630 --> 01:16:41.520
a regular version, it
can deform it as well.

01:16:41.520 --> 01:16:44.890
So now that we call that a prion.

01:16:44.890 --> 01:16:46.790
And then that can deform another protein

01:16:46.790 --> 01:16:47.780
and so on and so forth.

01:16:47.780 --> 01:16:52.510
So that can lead to diseases,
like mad cow disease

01:16:52.510 --> 01:16:57.510
that people have gotten from
eating contaminated beef

01:16:58.530 --> 01:17:01.840
or lamb, deer suffer from a prion

01:17:01.840 --> 01:17:05.120
called Chronic Wasting Disease,
which is a big problem.

01:17:05.120 --> 01:17:07.400
Deer hunters are aware of that.

01:17:07.400 --> 01:17:12.140
But again, it is just a
protein that it can reproduce.

01:17:13.370 --> 01:17:17.203
Well, it doesn't make a
new protein from scratch.

01:17:18.130 --> 01:17:19.433
Neither does the virus.

01:17:21.140 --> 01:17:24.770
All the SARs-Cov-2 in
the world is made by us,

01:17:24.770 --> 01:17:28.330
it's made out of human molecules.

01:17:28.330 --> 01:17:30.210
And so, it's sort of weird,

01:17:30.210 --> 01:17:31.560
like we think of this thing as a virus,

01:17:31.560 --> 01:17:34.033
but it's manufactured out of human parts.

01:17:35.290 --> 01:17:37.210
So I don't know.

01:17:37.210 --> 01:17:42.160
So, it's is making another
molecule take your shape?

01:17:42.160 --> 01:17:44.540
Is that what we call that reproduction?

01:17:44.540 --> 01:17:47.160
It's very hard to say

01:17:47.160 --> 01:17:49.610
and yet it has some lifelike properties.

01:17:49.610 --> 01:17:54.171
So again, it's not really the answer isn't

01:17:54.171 --> 01:17:56.730
maybe we shouldn't be
thinking sort of either or.

01:17:56.730 --> 01:17:58.400
I know we like to think of life or death,

01:17:58.400 --> 01:17:59.480
something's alive or it's not,

01:17:59.480 --> 01:18:02.064
but we have these very fuzzy cases

01:18:02.064 --> 01:18:05.023
that are interesting
because they're so fuzzy.

01:18:06.010 --> 01:18:06.843
Right.

01:18:07.790 --> 01:18:08.623
- Thank you.

01:18:08.623 --> 01:18:11.960
So the next question is
from Steven, who asked,

01:18:11.960 --> 01:18:14.730
it seems that exceptions
have been the most helpful

01:18:14.730 --> 01:18:16.990
in trying to establish a rule.

01:18:16.990 --> 01:18:19.990
How do the characteristics
of mitochondria support

01:18:19.990 --> 01:18:23.030
or fail to support their
candidacy for life,

01:18:23.030 --> 01:18:25.503
especially given their supposed origins?

01:18:27.130 --> 01:18:31.192
- Yeah, so, I could have spent,

01:18:31.192 --> 01:18:33.220
the hour talking about mitochondria

01:18:33.220 --> 01:18:34.800
instead of tardigrades or viruses,

01:18:34.800 --> 01:18:38.010
'cause here we have
another fascinating example

01:18:38.969 --> 01:18:40.913
are mitochondria alive?

01:18:45.050 --> 01:18:48.120
We probably all agreed
that their ancestors

01:18:48.120 --> 01:18:50.470
2 billion years ago were alive,

01:18:50.470 --> 01:18:52.590
in the sense that other bacteria

01:18:52.590 --> 01:18:55.713
floating in the ocean are alive.

01:18:59.731 --> 01:19:04.143
They were able to metabolize
and reproduce and evolve.

01:19:05.060 --> 01:19:07.730
What's kind of amazing is that
actually inside ourselves,

01:19:07.730 --> 01:19:09.957
still mitochondria divide,

01:19:09.957 --> 01:19:12.124
still mitochondria divide,

01:19:16.130 --> 01:19:18.483
they're sort of like
doing their own thing.

01:19:19.760 --> 01:19:22.890
The mitochondrial
division and cell division

01:19:22.890 --> 01:19:25.620
are kind of tied together but not totally.

01:19:25.620 --> 01:19:28.300
So different cells have
different numbers of mitochondria

01:19:28.300 --> 01:19:30.803
in them, which is interesting.

01:19:32.010 --> 01:19:33.610
So they're still doing

01:19:33.610 --> 01:19:36.430
part of what we think of as being alive.

01:19:36.430 --> 01:19:38.560
But the fact is that if
you look under the hood,

01:19:38.560 --> 01:19:41.943
if you open up my
mitochondria and look inside,

01:19:43.170 --> 01:19:46.513
they still have some genes.

01:19:47.440 --> 01:19:49.580
I forget the number,
something like 30 genes.

01:19:49.580 --> 01:19:51.430
So they have some DNA inside of them.

01:19:52.270 --> 01:19:54.073
And those genes produce proteins

01:19:54.073 --> 01:19:56.443
And those genes produce proteins

01:19:56.443 --> 01:19:58.860
that do incredibly important things,

01:19:58.860 --> 01:20:01.593
we get really sick of those
genes that are mutated.

01:20:04.410 --> 01:20:09.330
Those genes descend from
the genes that were carried

01:20:09.330 --> 01:20:10.963
by free living mitochondria.

01:20:12.280 --> 01:20:17.280
But those mitochondria had
thousands of other genes.

01:20:19.467 --> 01:20:21.240
So where'd they go, well,
some of them were lost

01:20:21.240 --> 01:20:23.050
that just didn't need them anymore.

01:20:23.050 --> 01:20:25.840
Some of them actually ended up getting

01:20:25.840 --> 01:20:30.840
moved from the mitochondria
into our own chromosomes,

01:20:30.840 --> 01:20:32.620
into our own DNA.

01:20:32.620 --> 01:20:34.700
So we have some, and
along with a viral DNA,

01:20:34.700 --> 01:20:36.900
we have some bacterial
DNA in there as well.

01:20:38.749 --> 01:20:41.349
So you have this thing like
if you take mitochondria

01:20:42.340 --> 01:20:44.410
and pluck them out of a
cell and just put them

01:20:44.410 --> 01:20:46.163
in a Petri dish and say like, okay,

01:20:47.250 --> 01:20:50.470
on your own, like they're dead.

01:20:50.470 --> 01:20:51.320
They can't do it.

01:20:52.810 --> 01:20:56.870
And yet there's nothing more important

01:20:56.870 --> 01:20:58.823
to our own life than mitochondria.

01:20:59.800 --> 01:21:02.920
You shut mitochondria down, we're dead.

01:21:02.920 --> 01:21:04.850
Some of the most potent poisons

01:21:04.850 --> 01:21:07.320
work by shutting down mitochondria.

01:21:07.320 --> 01:21:12.320
So, I have no idea if you called
mitochondria alive or not,

01:21:13.350 --> 01:21:15.550
it's really up to you, but
it's really interesting

01:21:15.550 --> 01:21:17.870
to think about how it compares to things

01:21:17.870 --> 01:21:21.323
that we're more comfortable calling alive.

01:21:23.300 --> 01:21:27.660
- Thanks, Carl, I think true
to your book's title, right?

01:21:27.660 --> 01:21:30.270
There are many questions
that one can ask about

01:21:30.270 --> 01:21:35.270
the edge of what counts as life
and how we define that edge.

01:21:35.600 --> 01:21:39.100
But before we close
this wonderful session,

01:21:39.100 --> 01:21:42.080
it is too tempting to let
you go before asking you

01:21:42.080 --> 01:21:47.080
what you think is coming next with COVID

01:21:47.370 --> 01:21:52.370
with our pandemic, are we
in for another or minor wave

01:21:54.490 --> 01:21:57.123
unnoticeable wave, noticeable wave?

01:21:58.480 --> 01:22:01.370
- Well, we're two years into this pandemic

01:22:01.370 --> 01:22:06.370
and I find that the smartest
scientists I talk to

01:22:07.140 --> 01:22:09.120
are also the most humble.

01:22:09.120 --> 01:22:13.785
They really respect this
viruses capacity to surprise us.

01:22:13.785 --> 01:22:18.590
Nobody saw Omicron for example coming,

01:22:18.590 --> 01:22:20.300
like no one had said like
we're gonna have something like

01:22:20.300 --> 01:22:22.933
Omicron until we had Omicron.

01:22:24.030 --> 01:22:26.970
So, people keep saying like,
oh, that's it we're done.

01:22:26.970 --> 01:22:29.900
Or, "Oh, virus has always
evolved to get milder"

01:22:29.900 --> 01:22:34.680
Oh, the virus has run
as far as it can go."

01:22:34.680 --> 01:22:38.320
And I honestly don't think
that we know that or not,

01:22:38.320 --> 01:22:43.160
and that's not a happy,
that's not a happy thought,

01:22:43.160 --> 01:22:45.450
given all the suffering
that it's caused us already.

01:22:45.450 --> 01:22:50.450
But I, this right now, today
as we speak there is this

01:22:54.810 --> 01:22:59.810
sub variant, a sub variant
of Omicron called BA2,

01:23:00.310 --> 01:23:04.380
which is has become dominant.

01:23:04.380 --> 01:23:07.340
Most cases in the United States are that,

01:23:07.340 --> 01:23:10.090
but cases are still pretty low in the US.

01:23:10.090 --> 01:23:15.090
And, it's possible that this
is going to give us a bump.

01:23:16.130 --> 01:23:18.640
But on the other hand a lot of people

01:23:18.640 --> 01:23:20.510
have already been infected recently

01:23:20.510 --> 01:23:23.680
or they've been vaccinated
so they may reign that in.

01:23:26.520 --> 01:23:28.120
I don't really think we know.

01:23:28.120 --> 01:23:30.640
I mean, we will know in
a week or in two weeks

01:23:30.640 --> 01:23:33.730
about this one, but my question is

01:23:33.730 --> 01:23:35.302
what happens in six months?

01:23:35.302 --> 01:23:36.730
- Yeah.

01:23:36.730 --> 01:23:38.870
- It seems like every
six months or something,

01:23:38.870 --> 01:23:43.870
we get an Alpha, a Delta, and
Omicron, a really big thing.

01:23:44.520 --> 01:23:46.900
Are we gonna be due for a new one?

01:23:46.900 --> 01:23:48.330
There's certainly lots
of viruses out there

01:23:48.330 --> 01:23:53.330
that are evolving, so
there might be another one,

01:23:53.490 --> 01:23:54.860
there might be another one in the works.

01:23:54.860 --> 01:23:59.860
And, if we're, if things work out for us,

01:24:00.690 --> 01:24:03.533
our vaccines will continue
to protect us against it.

01:24:04.580 --> 01:24:09.543
But, if we sort of dropped the
ball on vaccine development,

01:24:11.170 --> 01:24:16.170
we could be looking at a
new form of SARs-Cov-2 that,

01:24:16.258 --> 01:24:18.980
our vaccines are just
not really good against.

01:24:18.980 --> 01:24:23.980
So it's been a long two
years, but I just don't,

01:24:24.230 --> 01:24:27.363
I see no reason to just get complacent.

01:24:28.840 --> 01:24:32.778
- Gosh, sounds like we
need to be conscious,

01:24:32.778 --> 01:24:37.778
and this pandemic could
just ooze on its way out.

01:24:38.550 --> 01:24:42.540
And maybe, I guess the,
well let's not leave it

01:24:42.540 --> 01:24:44.860
on a pessimistic note though,

01:24:44.860 --> 01:24:49.310
we might have this with
us forever, but anyway,

01:24:49.310 --> 01:24:53.010
thank you so much Carl
for taking the time,

01:24:53.010 --> 01:24:57.710
to talk to us today and thank
you for all the wonderful work

01:24:57.710 --> 01:25:02.710
that you do, making the
science in particular

01:25:03.060 --> 01:25:07.020
during this pandemic
intelligible to the rest of us.

